WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.800
I'll explain where we
are in the whole course.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:15.800
In the next maybe 2 and
1/2, 3 weeks, we build up.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.130
And we're going from the
bottom up to the top.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:20.810
So this will finish
when you'll actually

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.692
implement your own solver, an LP
solver, or something like that.

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:26.900
We haven't quite figured out.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.740
But anyway, it'll finish with
you implementing a solver,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.670
so mostly, by the way,
just to demystify it,

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.790
just so when someone looks
at you sometime in the future

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.787
and says, oh my god, you can't
write your own LP solver.

00:00:40.787 --> 00:00:41.870
That's really complicated.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.730
And you could say, au
contraire, I did it.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:45.230
Right?

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.080
So that's the main
thing, the main reason.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.510
But also there's
interesting stuff.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.410
Right now we're at the bottom
level, which is linear algebra.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:56.360
And actually, in some
ways, it's by far

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.890
the most important level.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:02.720
And my own feeling is
certainly, anybody in this class

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.980
needs to know this material.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.440
Do you need to know a
whole 10-week course

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.590
that you would take on
computational linear algebra?

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.630
Hmm, maybe, actually.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:17.550
But certainly, you need to
be familiar with the ideas.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.100
And that's unlike--
you don't have

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.630
to know about
computer architecture,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:25.440
even though everything we
do is thanks to the people

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.210
who design and build
computers and stuff.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.030
So but this you
actually should know.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.250
OK, so back to the numerical
linear algebra-- and today,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.605
actually, we're going to cover
material that some of you

00:01:41.605 --> 00:01:42.105
may know.

00:01:42.105 --> 00:01:43.870
The rest of you probably don't.

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.890
And I've listed in the
top 10 of the things

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.640
you need to know
from this course,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.370
even though it's not
technically part of the course.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:52.320
So OK.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.130
So here it is.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.620
Last time we talked
about, how do you solve

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:59.350
a set of equations Ax equals b?

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.578
And there are several ways.

00:02:01.578 --> 00:02:03.370
There's ones we're not
going to talk about.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.530
There are iterative
methods to do

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.550
this, which are used for super
large-scale problems, if you've

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.250
got like a billion variables
or if that represents

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:13.780
a PDE you're solving.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:14.560
Right?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.250
We're not going to
talk about those.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.792
We're going to talk
about-- these are

00:02:17.792 --> 00:02:19.750
what are called direct methods.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.380
I don't know where
that name came from,

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.270
but they're called direct.

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:24.325
And the way they work is
they take the matrix A,

00:02:24.325 --> 00:02:28.720
and they first factor it
into a product of matrices,

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.890
each of which, quote, "is
easy to invert," unquote.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.065
So that's the first step, right?

00:02:35.065 --> 00:02:37.240
And you've seen some of
these factorizations,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:39.520
like SVD, eigendecomposition.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.220
You've probably seen a
couple of the others as well.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.480
So an easy to invert
is actually just--

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.130
that's dialect for it's
easy to solve equations

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:52.390
with that coefficient matrix.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:53.682
So that's what it really means.

00:02:53.682 --> 00:02:55.990
Because no one is
inverting anything, right?

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:01.280
It is unbelievably rare in
good numerical work for anybody

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.290
to actually form the
inverse of a matrix.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:07.310
There are cases where people
do, but it's very rare.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:12.050
So OK, so what you
do then, once you've

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:16.075
factored your matrix into a
product of things which are,

00:03:16.075 --> 00:03:17.450
quote, "easy to
invert," unquote,

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:21.110
you have to interpret that
correctly, then what you do

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.450
is you then write
out the inverse.

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:26.390
And the inverse is the
product of the inverses

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:27.410
in reverse order.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.290
And then you literally
just evaluate this

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:33.190
by first evaluating
A1 inverse b.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:33.980
That's one thing.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:35.480
And then, of course,
what that means

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.280
is people write A1 inverse b.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.240
What they really mean is
you call solve A1, comma b.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.560
That's what it really means.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:44.900
So you solve that.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.150
Then you pass this
solution to the next thing,

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.860
which is A2 inverse times x1.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.610
And so you solve A2x2 equals x1.

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.080
And that gives you
the second one.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.920
And you keep going until
you get to the end.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.120
So again, it's completely
straightforward.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:06.170
Like, OK, so what?

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.040
Last time, we talked about some
very interesting things, which

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.120
is that if you want to
solve a set of equations

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:15.860
with the same
coefficient matrix,

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.029
but different right-hand sides,
then in fact, what you can do

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:24.230
is you factor once, and you
cache the factorization.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.410
And then you simply
use the solve method.

00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:29.010
This is called the solve.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:32.330
The first one is called factor,
and this is called solve.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:34.190
So you simply call
the solve method

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.380
on your multiple
right-hand sides.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:44.120
And you get to surprising
conclusions in some cases.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:45.330
I'll get to that in a minute.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:48.620
So all right, so this is
the one that you probably

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.650
saw a variation of this in
high school or something,

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.660
the first time you
saw linear equations.

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.310
It's called Gaussian
elimination.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:57.150
It's called lots of things.

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.350
But here's what it is.

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:00.910
If you have a
non-singular matrix,

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:04.510
you can factor it as a
permutation times a lower

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.880
triangular matrix.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:09.475
It's a lower triangular,
and U is upper triangular.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:10.600
And they're all invertible.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:11.830
Of course, actually,
if any one of them

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.330
weren't, then they would not be.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.620
Right.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:17.800
So the cost of
that factorization,

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.935
which we're not going to
go into how that's done.

00:05:19.935 --> 00:05:21.310
It's actually not
that difficult.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.870
But the cost is order
n cubed flops, OK?

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.590
So that's what that is.

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:31.060
And so this is what it looks
like to solve linear equations.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:37.600
You factor A. That means
you get P, L, and U. Then

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.300
these three steps, two,
three, four, this is factor,

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.690
and then this is solve.

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:46.120
So again, all you do is you just
solve them in reverse order.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:49.730
Now, solving this one, solving
the permutation, this is silly.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:52.532
It's multiply by P
transpose, roughly.

00:05:52.532 --> 00:05:53.740
It's the inverse permutation.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.900
You unpermute them, right?

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:57.470
So this is easy.

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.650
The second one, L
is lower triangular.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.280
And so for that, you use
forward substitution,

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.250
which we saw as an order
n squared algorithm.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:07.350
Backward substitution
is when you have U

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:08.730
because U is upper triangular.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:10.710
And you start, if
you will recall,

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:12.563
by first solving for
the last entry then,

00:06:12.563 --> 00:06:14.730
then the second last, and
the third last, and so on.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.310
That's backward
substitution, and that's also

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.150
n squared flops.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.490
And if you add these
up, what you find out

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:21.900
is something kind of weird.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.000
It's that the factor
is order n cubed,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.560
and the solve is
order n squared.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.060
And so I think last
time, I finished

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:30.930
by asking you a question,
which is, how long does it

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.560
take to solve, I don't
know, on my laptop,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.980
a set of linear equations
with, let's say, a 1,000

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.120
by 1,000 matrix?

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:39.690
Right?

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.150
Which I could make
sound pretty impressive.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:46.770
I could say, my problem has
more than a million coefficients

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.850
in it, which is true.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:50.590
1,000 by 1,000 matrix does.

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:54.380
And then you could
say, the n cubed,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:56.900
the factorization
is order n cubed.

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:00.400
That's a billion
flops, Right Of course,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.740
that's a joke now, right?

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.150
And the answer is, I can do
that in 100 milliseconds.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:08.020
OK?

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:08.860
So can you.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.230
My phone can do it in
something on that order

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:15.940
of magnitude, which is
actually kind of stunning.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.190
I think then I asked
the following question.

00:07:18.190 --> 00:07:23.180
I asked, if it takes 100
milliseconds to solve,

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:27.400
let's say, one set of
1,000 by 1,000 equations,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:29.290
1,000 equations, 1,000
unknowns, how long

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:30.910
does it take to solve 10?

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.580
For that matter,
even 100, how long

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:37.030
does it take, if it takes a
tenth of a second to do one?

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:38.380
And the answer is surprising.

00:07:38.380 --> 00:07:39.100
It's this.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.630
It's a tenth of a second.

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:41.620
Everybody got that?

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.110
So not that many
people appreciate this

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.220
or understand this.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:46.810
And this is actually--
this is used

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.600
throughout a lot
of numerical stuff.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.010
A lot of it, well, you
use it all the time.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:53.060
You just don't know it.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.150
So all the solvers
that you use when

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.470
you call CVXPY almost all of
them will use things like this.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:03.850
So the most typical methods to
solve things like SOCPs, SDPs,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:04.540
these things.

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:08.830
Or for that matter, even LPs
involve two solves, right?

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:12.910
And basically, the
factorization dominates,

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.500
and that's the point there.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.000
OK?

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.057
So all right, actually,
I'm just curious.

00:08:18.057 --> 00:08:19.640
How many people have
seen this before?

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.750
How many people knew that
you could solve 10 equations

00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:26.950
with 1,000 variables, 1,000
unknowns, same coefficient

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.843
matrix, different
right-hand sides,

00:08:29.843 --> 00:08:31.510
you could solve 10
in the amount of time

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.250
it takes you to solve one?

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:34.330
You knew?

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.590
Come on, [? say. ?]
Other people?

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.710
Yeah, is no one else impressed?

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:41.370
Or shouldn't you be--

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:44.190
I mean, it's maybe
too early, but people

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.920
should be going, like,
that's awesome, really?

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:48.557
But I'm not getting
that feeling.

00:08:48.557 --> 00:08:49.140
But that's OK.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:50.280
That's fine.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:51.840
OK, good, there we go.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:57.580
All right, now
sparse factorization,

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.320
this is super interesting.

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.980
This is something everybody
should know about.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:07.440
So I guess it's been developed
since about the 1960s, is

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.290
basically, stuff
on sparse matrices.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:14.610
And so first of all, what
are the data structures you

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:15.840
use to store them?

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.162
And then, basically, there's
a whole parallel universe

00:09:18.162 --> 00:09:19.620
of doing all the
stuff you would do

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.350
with so-called dense
matrices, which are just

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.210
stored as arrays, right?

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:25.890
How do you do this
for sparse matrices,

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.040
including factorizations,
back solves,

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.220
all these kinds of things?

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:33.210
Everything you can do,
eigendecomposition, singular

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.670
value decomposition,
there's a whole field

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.940
where people have made this
efficient for sparse matrices.

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:44.220
And this is something everybody
needs to know about, everyone.

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:46.470
Because if you do any
kind of computation,

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.910
you need to know about this.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:52.170
Because, again, there will
be surprising statements

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.660
we'll make about what you
can solve and not solve.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:10:00.580
OK, so if A is sparse,
the typical factorization

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.680
is going to have four factors.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.240
It's going to have a P1,
is a permutation in front.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.130
P2 is a permutation in back.

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:14.200
And then L and U are
the lower triangular

00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:16.760
and upper triangular.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.260
So the idea here
is mathematically,

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.600
you don't need P2.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.220
You do need P1.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:27.520
It is false that any
non-singular matrix

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:29.630
can be factored as LU.

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:30.130
OK?

00:10:30.130 --> 00:10:32.170
You do need that
initial permutation.

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:34.070
But you don't need
that second one.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:35.710
So the second one is there.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.860
And by the way,
P1 is also there,

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:43.350
so that when you carry
out this LU factorization,

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.210
L and U will also be sparse.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:48.130
Because if you do
this, if you're not

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:51.220
careful about doing this,
your matrix A will be sparse,

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:53.730
but L and U will be dense.

00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:56.670
When that happens, it's
referred to as you have fill-in.

00:10:56.670 --> 00:10:59.280
You have lots of entries,
entries in L and U

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.950
that weren't there in A. OK?

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:05.580
And this is very important
for everybody to know, right?

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.100
And here would be an example.

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.750
It's extremely common to work
all the time with, let's say,

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:15.120
a million by million
sparse matrix.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:18.210
It is simply not
a problem, right?

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.720
I can do stuff like that
on my laptop, right?

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.970
The number of
non-zeros is not going

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:27.475
to be 10 to the 12, which
would be a dense one.

00:11:27.475 --> 00:11:29.850
It's going to be something
reasonable like, I don't know,

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.825
it's got 20, 30, 100
non-zeros per row.

00:11:32.825 --> 00:11:34.200
Even that is not
that big a deal,

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.530
because it's 100 million
coefficients if there's

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.120
100 non-zeros per row.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:40.990
And you multiply
that by about 10.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:45.540
So even in doubles, it's a
gig, which is just very small.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.590
It's a reasonably
small amount of memory.

00:11:47.590 --> 00:11:48.090
OK?

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:52.530
So anyway, so what happens
is if you have your million

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:55.720
by million matrix, and you
call a solver like this,

00:11:55.720 --> 00:12:01.480
if you choose P1 and P2
poorly, then what will happen

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:05.410
is L will be like a dense
lower triangular matrix.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.140
You can't even store it, because
it's a million by million.

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.630
So it's 10 to the 12
divided by 2 things.

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.280
I mean, you can't.

00:12:12.280 --> 00:12:14.620
Let's just leave
it that way, OK?

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:16.570
Making sense?

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:19.240
So the P1 and P2, they're
chosen heuristically.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:21.550
And people have been
doing this since the '60s.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:22.760
It's actually very cool.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:27.460
You can ask questions like,
oh, find me the P1 and P2

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:32.570
that minimize the number of
non-zeros in L and U. That's

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:34.550
a perfectly nice
problem to come up with.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:37.970
And it is NP-hard, OK?

00:12:37.970 --> 00:12:38.700
But who cares?

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:39.200
It's silly.

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:41.900
There are unbelievably
good heuristics

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:46.100
for doing this that, I mean,
all of us benefit from.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.560
So this is great.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:49.160
OK.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:53.060
And so what happens with
sparse matrices is the factor

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:57.740
and solve times,
whatever they are,

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:00.480
they are less than 2/3 n cubed.

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.490
And I'm going to give you a
very important special case

00:13:04.490 --> 00:13:08.300
right now, and then
we'll encounter others.

00:13:08.300 --> 00:13:11.180
So here's a super
important special case.

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:13.730
It's a so-called banded matrix.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:16.490
So a banded matrix
looks like this.

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:19.580
Well, of course, there's
just a band here, like this.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:22.880
Maybe this is k
entries wide, right?

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:26.750
And basically, this is non-zero,
and everything out here

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:28.340
is-- that's zero, and that's 0.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.890
So that's a banded
matrix, right?

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:35.420
So I can store a banded
matrix on my laptop easily

00:13:35.420 --> 00:13:37.890
if it's a million by million
and the bandwidth is,

00:13:37.890 --> 00:13:40.940
let's say, 10 or 20
right so everybody

00:13:40.940 --> 00:13:43.640
so that's a banded matrix.

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:44.300
That's fine.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:50.360
Well, this is a case where
you don't need P1 and P2.

00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:54.320
But if you simply do the
LU factorization here,

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:58.417
the L and the U are going
to be, basically, the same.

00:13:58.417 --> 00:13:59.750
They're also going to be banded.

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:04.190
So I'll just write down L.
L Is lower triangular, so--

00:14:04.190 --> 00:14:04.760
whoops.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:06.380
This goes down to there, right?

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.840
And then it simply
looks like that.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:10.580
That's k over 2.

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.860
And this is what L looks like.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:15.290
And U looks similarly, right?

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:18.858
So by the way, sometimes,
a banded matrix

00:14:18.858 --> 00:14:20.150
comes to you looking like that.

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:21.560
But sometimes,
it's been permuted

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:24.255
by whoever formed the problem.

00:14:24.255 --> 00:14:26.630
They decide, well, I'm going
to list all my states first,

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:28.880
then all my controls,
then all this,

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:30.740
in an optimal control problem.

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.720
And then it comes out with
some crazy sparsity pattern.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:39.300
And any methods used to
heuristically choose P1 and P2

00:14:39.300 --> 00:14:41.580
will permute it to look banded.

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:42.120
OK.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.720
So what happens in a banded
factorization is, first of all,

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:51.630
the factorization
time is nk squared,

00:14:51.630 --> 00:14:55.630
and then the back solve is nk.

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:56.140
OK?

00:14:56.140 --> 00:15:02.350
So these are stunningly smaller
than n cubed and n squared.

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:02.860
OK?

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:08.440
So what this tells you is
you can solve a million

00:15:08.440 --> 00:15:13.900
by million equations if the
bandwidth is 10 super easily.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:16.360
And it's going to be so
fast, it's not even funny.

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.070
Everybody following this?

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:19.112
Which is shocking, right?

00:15:19.112 --> 00:15:20.820
Because someone said,
what are you doing?

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:22.960
You say, I'm solving a
set of a million equations

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.460
for a million variables.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.430
Each equation involves
10 of the variables.

00:15:27.430 --> 00:15:29.890
And you're like, whoa,
that sounds like it's hard.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:30.640
Right?

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:32.560
The answer is,
it's unbelievable.

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.330
This is a millisecond operation.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:38.230
Everyone [? follow? ?] It's
just this is completely insane.

00:15:38.230 --> 00:15:40.780
You absolutely
need to know this,

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.570
because this is the trick
to many, many things.

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:44.920
OK.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:47.800
So by the way,
what's interesting

00:15:47.800 --> 00:15:51.520
here is the factor solve ratio
is not that high anymore.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:52.270
Now it's k.

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:53.680
It used to be n.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:56.390
And so that says, if I
say, how long does it

00:15:56.390 --> 00:15:58.910
take to solve a million by
million equations banded

00:15:58.910 --> 00:16:00.050
with a bandwidth of 10?

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.780
It's some number which
is shockingly small.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:04.320
Then you say, how
about 10 of them?

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:04.820
OK.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:05.810
How about two of them?

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:08.210
And you say, it's only
a little, tiny bit more.

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:09.630
Everybody following this?

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:10.130
OK.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:14.720
So by the way, what you'll be
doing in the next week, two

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:20.330
weeks, is you will start to
recognize matrix structure,

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:21.140
like sparsity.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:23.810
You're going to see some
others later today, like block

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:26.240
arrow and weird other ones.

00:16:26.240 --> 00:16:27.890
And L will start
recognizing them.

00:16:27.890 --> 00:16:31.020
They're super important
because two things.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:34.670
Number one, you'll start
recognizing structures

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.430
that you can exploit to
solve extremely fast.

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:38.750
That's number one.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.670
But number two,
on the other end,

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:42.440
you're going to
start recognizing

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:46.160
practical problems that
will result in matrices

00:16:46.160 --> 00:16:47.120
with that structure.

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:48.800
I'm going to mention
one right now.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:53.330
All of control, all
of signal processing,

00:16:53.330 --> 00:16:57.320
just looks like that,
every last bit of it, OK?

00:16:57.320 --> 00:17:00.620
So that means if I ask
you to-- and you've

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.930
solved problems, little control
problems, and things like that.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:06.098
But what this says
is, if someone says,

00:17:06.098 --> 00:17:06.890
I have to compute--

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:10.700
I have to solve a control
problem with 20,000 time

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:14.545
steps and a state dimension
of 20 and 10 actuators,

00:17:14.545 --> 00:17:15.920
and someone say,
wow, that sounds

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:17.460
like it's going
to be complicated,

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:21.290
you just laugh and
say, are you kidding?

00:17:21.290 --> 00:17:23.150
I can do that on my phone.

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.940
And, of course, that's the
key to actually putting

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:28.190
these things into a
lot of control systems.

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:30.270
So a lot of them
actually do this.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.325
So OK, so that's
the big picture.

00:17:32.325 --> 00:17:38.660
So this is just super
cool to know about.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:39.920
OK.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:43.470
This brings us to a very
famous factorization.

00:17:43.470 --> 00:17:45.320
it's about 100 years old.

00:17:45.320 --> 00:17:47.990
It's the Cholesky factorization.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:51.260
So if you have a
positive definite matrix,

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:53.570
you can write it as
just LL transpose.

00:17:53.570 --> 00:17:57.390
And if you insist that
the diagonal entries of L

00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:00.150
are positive, then it's unique.

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:02.250
So there's no choice.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:04.230
There's just one
factorization, period.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:05.130
That's it.

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:08.370
There's one way to
write A as LL transpose.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:12.540
And how do you
solve Ax equals b,

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.430
where A is positive definite?

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.830
That's called a positive
definite system of equations.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:17.890
So how do you do that?

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.680
You do a Cholesky factorization.

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:22.290
And you do a forward and a
backward substitution with L

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:24.360
and L transpose, OK?

00:18:24.360 --> 00:18:25.500
So that's it.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:29.370
And actually, it's 1/3 n
cubed, as opposed to 2n cubed,

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:30.480
for general matrices.

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.400
And that makes sense.

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.770
Number one, no one
cares about a factor

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:37.140
of 2 in a flop count
estimate, because these

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:38.940
are so crude, right?

00:18:38.940 --> 00:18:41.150
What really matters
is the people

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:43.650
who actually wrote the microcode
and all that kind of stuff.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:44.640
And how is it blocked?

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:47.460
And how many cores is it
using, all sorts of crazy stuff

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:50.190
internal, that most of
us don't-- all of us,

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:53.620
I'm guessing, should not and
do not have to worry about.

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:54.120
Right?

00:18:54.120 --> 00:18:56.340
But we should, also,
all be very grateful

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:57.900
that there are people who do.

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:00.210
Right?

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:02.370
Yeah, so it's a factor
of 2, which is not

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:03.510
unsurprisingly symmetric.

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:06.630
So roughly speaking,
there's a redundancy of 2.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:09.100
Or you can even look
at this and say, hey,

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:14.177
that's an LU factorization with
U equals L. And why, you say?

00:19:14.177 --> 00:19:15.510
Because the matrix is symmetric.

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:16.620
That's why.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:20.160
So it's not too surprising.

00:19:20.160 --> 00:19:25.380
We will see very shortly where
positive definite systems

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:26.880
of equations arise.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:28.620
Actually, they
arise by themselves

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:29.830
in a lot of applications.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:33.000
But specifically, in
convex optimization,

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:36.030
we'll see they're at the
heart of a lot of stuff.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:37.230
OK?

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:40.050
And now we get the sparse
Cholesky factorization.

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:42.450
And here, I'll tell
you something super

00:19:42.450 --> 00:19:44.220
interesting about it, I think.

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:47.220
So here, it turns out
this is, basically,

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:52.123
the same as calling a Cholesky
factorization on P transpose AP

00:19:52.123 --> 00:19:54.540
because take that equation,
just multiplying the left by P

00:19:54.540 --> 00:19:58.340
transpose, on the right by
P. So what it basically says

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:01.980
is, permute your matrix, your
positive definite matrix,

00:20:01.980 --> 00:20:04.970
and then do a Cholesky
factorization, where

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.620
there is no creativity.

00:20:06.620 --> 00:20:09.140
It's just there's a unique
Cholesky factorization.

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:10.370
Everybody got this?

00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:13.670
When you do that, you will
find some stunning things out.

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:18.260
You will find out, that if
you choose a good permutation,

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:21.500
the L is going to
be super sparse.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:25.010
If you choose a bad permutation,
it's going to be dense.

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:29.910
And I'll give you a
famous example of this.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:31.790
So it would be
something like this.

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:36.960
If I gave you this matrix here--

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:38.840
so I'm going to draw.

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:40.640
Here, I'll just
make it this way.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:43.860
So it's got first row and
column and a diagonal.

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:44.360
OK?

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:47.420
So by the way, that's called
an up-- what do they call it?

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:48.655
It's an arrow matrix, right?

00:20:48.655 --> 00:20:50.030
Because if you
blur your eyes, it

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:51.230
looks like an
arrow, or something.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:51.900
I don't know.

00:20:51.900 --> 00:20:54.560
Anyway, that's an
upper arrow matrix.

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:56.300
It's super sparse, right?

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:59.010
Number of non-zeros
per row and column,

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:02.400
except for the first row
and column, it's two, right?

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:09.360
So OK, so that's an example of a
structure you should never ever

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:13.240
see again and not have an
emotional response to ever,

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:13.740
right?

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:15.280
Because this is super important.

00:21:15.280 --> 00:21:15.780
OK?

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:21.090
So OK, if I do a Cholesky
factorization on that, here's--

00:21:21.090 --> 00:21:23.280
I'm going to show you the
sparsity pattern of L.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:24.100
It's this.

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:24.600
Ready?

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:29.170
It's completely dense,
there, completely dense.

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:29.670
OK?

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:32.472
So this, of course,
ruins everything, right?

00:21:32.472 --> 00:21:34.680
For one thing, you can't
even store that much, right?

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:36.222
Because if you
started with a million

00:21:36.222 --> 00:21:40.350
by million upper arrow
matrix, called Cholesky

00:21:40.350 --> 00:21:44.280
on it, at some point,
your OS would say,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.620
no, no, I am not
going to allocate

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:47.820
that amount of memory, no.

00:21:47.820 --> 00:21:49.720
So everybody got this?

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:50.220
OK.

00:21:50.220 --> 00:21:52.860
So now it turns out
all you need to do

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:58.507
with this is permute the
first and last columns,

00:21:58.507 --> 00:21:59.840
first and last rows and columns.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.640
And then you end up
with this matrix.

00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:07.418
And that, that's called a--

00:22:07.418 --> 00:22:07.960
I don't know.

00:22:07.960 --> 00:22:09.460
It's a whatever.

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:11.537
That's called an arrow
structure, right?

00:22:11.537 --> 00:22:12.370
We'll get to others.

00:22:12.370 --> 00:22:13.330
But anyway, that's one.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:16.690
OK, if I do a Cholesky
factorization on this,

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:20.095
I will show you the
sparsity pattern you get.

00:22:20.095 --> 00:22:25.250
You get this, and
that's all, OK?

00:22:25.250 --> 00:22:29.240
So what it means is--

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:33.350
it means that the permutation
that permuted this into this

00:22:33.350 --> 00:22:36.830
was super important, at least
from a sparsity point of view.

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:38.270
OK, everybody got it?

00:22:38.270 --> 00:22:41.810
So what this means is, if
you had that set of equations

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:44.330
to solve--

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:46.997
actually, if you just call a
sparse solver on your behalf,

00:22:46.997 --> 00:22:48.080
you wouldn't even know it.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:52.160
But the permutation would
change it to this, factor that.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:55.550
And then your factor time
is going to be order n,

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:58.865
and your solve time
will be order n.

00:22:58.865 --> 00:22:59.365
Right?

00:22:59.365 --> 00:23:02.170
So everybody following this?

00:23:02.170 --> 00:23:02.930
So that's it.

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:05.172
So fortunately for a lot
of those things, You.

00:23:05.172 --> 00:23:06.380
Don't have to worry about it.

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:07.540
That's all based inside.

00:23:07.540 --> 00:23:11.530
All of you have benefited from
this probably a million times

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:13.150
just so far in this class.

00:23:13.150 --> 00:23:16.660
Because every time
CVXPY calls a solver,

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:18.140
this is what's happening.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:19.890
This is the kind of
stuff that's happening

00:23:19.890 --> 00:23:22.030
on your behalf, many,
many times, every time

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:23.630
you call solve on something.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:24.130
OK?

00:23:24.130 --> 00:23:25.360
So it doesn't matter.

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.520
I mean, but just now you know.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:30.590
OK, this make sense?

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:31.730
Yeah?

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:34.160
How much time does it
take for the solver

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:37.022
to find that piece, that step?

00:23:37.022 --> 00:23:38.450
That's a perfect question.

00:23:38.450 --> 00:23:39.410
I should have said it.

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:41.870
So the question is, how long--

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:44.810
so yeah, if I say, oh,
man, are you kidding?

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:46.390
I can do this for
you, not a problem.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:48.140
I'm going to find a
permutation like that,

00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.040
and then I can solve this
in a tenth of a second.

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:50.870
And you go, cool.

00:23:50.870 --> 00:23:52.953
How long does it take you
to find the permutation?

00:23:52.953 --> 00:23:54.620
You go, hmm, a couple of days.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:57.740
It's just not-- yeah, so
the general rule of thumb

00:23:57.740 --> 00:24:03.200
is people use heuristics
for finding P that are,

00:24:03.200 --> 00:24:07.520
they hope, on the order of
magnitude of the amount of time

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:11.500
it takes to actually do the
numerical factorization.

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:12.580
So that's what they do.

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:16.240
Now, there are applications
where people will rum--

00:24:16.240 --> 00:24:19.420
they'll take more time
to actually work out

00:24:19.420 --> 00:24:22.270
a better permutation
because they're going

00:24:22.270 --> 00:24:24.450
to use this a gazillion times.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:24.950
OK?

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:26.980
So this would be the case
for embedded systems,

00:24:26.980 --> 00:24:28.150
like for control.

00:24:28.150 --> 00:24:28.690
Right?

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:32.030
So if you're doing control, and
you're going to put this into,

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:34.090
let's say, a rocket,
or something like that,

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:36.510
and you know it's
going to run at 100

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:39.760
hertz for the entire
mission, or whatever

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:43.360
it is, that's a lot of time.

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:45.880
You could definitely
take an evening

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:47.980
to come up with a
better permutation.

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:50.050
But this is not what
most people doing--

00:24:50.050 --> 00:24:54.040
most people who
work on the solution

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:56.500
of sparse linear
equations don't do that.

00:24:56.500 --> 00:24:59.650
They just stick with
heuristics that are fast.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.710
And by the way, that portion
is called the symbolic--

00:25:02.710 --> 00:25:04.960
sometimes, they call
it the symbolic solve

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:06.380
or the permutation phase.

00:25:06.380 --> 00:25:09.380
There's lots of names for it.

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:10.130
And you can tell.

00:25:14.300 --> 00:25:17.630
If you actually look at it to
see what it's actually doing,

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:20.210
you will find out that it's
spending a whole bunch of time.

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:22.670
And it's doing
graph calculations.

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:25.230
There's no floating
point numbers involved.

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:25.730
OK?

00:25:25.730 --> 00:25:28.220
And then when floating point
number operations start up,

00:25:28.220 --> 00:25:30.870
that means you're actually doing
the numerical factorization.

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:34.160
So that's a good question.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:35.720
OK.

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:37.460
Now, I know I'm not able to--

00:25:37.460 --> 00:25:39.200
I mean, I realize,
for many of you,

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:40.400
this is the first
time you've seen it.

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:40.850
That's fine.

00:25:40.850 --> 00:25:42.830
You should just get the
high-level picture of it.

00:25:42.830 --> 00:25:43.538
And you can read.

00:25:43.538 --> 00:25:46.730
There's a little bit more
in the appendix in the book.

00:25:46.730 --> 00:25:48.508
There's an appendix on this.

00:25:48.508 --> 00:25:50.550
And this is just something
you should know about.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:52.187
So I don't know, SCIPY.

00:25:52.187 --> 00:25:54.020
You could poke around
in SCIPY or something.

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:56.187
Once you know about this,
you can't unknow about it.

00:25:56.187 --> 00:25:58.490
And you will have
incredible powers.

00:25:58.490 --> 00:25:59.420
Yes?

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:01.370
What kind of heuristics?

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:06.650
Well, so some are
kind of greedy.

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:08.670
I'd have to tell you,
actually, how you actually

00:26:08.670 --> 00:26:13.050
do the factorization to
actually explain the heuristics.

00:26:13.050 --> 00:26:15.300
But a lot of them are just
constructed one by one.

00:26:15.300 --> 00:26:19.020
What they do is they say, P
is going to permute something

00:26:19.020 --> 00:26:20.160
to the top row.

00:26:20.160 --> 00:26:22.020
And they'd say,
I'm going to find

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.420
which entry I should
permute to the top,

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:26.910
then who gets permuted
to the second top, then

00:26:26.910 --> 00:26:29.550
third top, and fourth
top, things like that.

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:30.960
But that's greedy.

00:26:30.960 --> 00:26:32.668
But that's the kind
of things people use.

00:26:32.668 --> 00:26:34.460
And they have lots of
names, and it's worth

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:35.860
knowing a little bit about them.

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:36.467
Yeah?

00:26:36.467 --> 00:26:38.550
So this wouldn't be hard
for finding the best one,

00:26:38.550 --> 00:26:40.925
but are there like approximation
guarantees [INAUDIBLE]??

00:26:40.925 --> 00:26:44.130
No, there's no approximation
guarantees whatsoever, no.

00:26:44.130 --> 00:26:46.410
So yeah, no one has-- yeah.

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:51.600
So it's one of those
things where we can ask--

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.990
of course, these are
all graph calculations.

00:26:54.990 --> 00:26:57.600
These are just these are just
questions about graphs, right?

00:26:57.600 --> 00:26:59.140
Oh, there is one exception.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:00.960
There's one theory of it.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:01.710
That's this.

00:27:01.710 --> 00:27:04.770
If the graph represented by
the sparsity pattern of A

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:11.630
is chordal, then
it turns out it's

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:13.790
guaranteed there exists
a permutation which

00:27:13.790 --> 00:27:17.480
has zero fill-in, meaning
the number of non-zeros in L

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:22.340
will be identical to the number
of non-zeros in A divided by 2.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:25.760
So if you know what a chordal
graph, that's one of them.

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:27.677
That's actually useless,
frankly, in practice.

00:27:27.677 --> 00:27:30.093
Because the real question is
not whether there exists one.

00:27:30.093 --> 00:27:32.130
The question is whether
our heuristics find it.

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:36.980
And the answer is, all decent
heuristics will find that one.

00:27:36.980 --> 00:27:38.270
Yeah?

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:40.730
Do each of these
factorizations require

00:27:40.730 --> 00:27:43.280
that we know the matrix
is non-singular, that it's

00:27:43.280 --> 00:27:46.010
positive definite [INAUDIBLE]?

00:27:46.010 --> 00:27:49.790
So it turns out in
this one, to find

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:52.250
the permutation for
Cholesky factorization,

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:55.640
you actually don't have to know
the entries in A. You simply

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:57.560
have to know their locations.

00:27:57.560 --> 00:28:00.200
That's actually a super
important, more advanced.

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:02.120
But you brought it up.

00:28:02.120 --> 00:28:05.552
So what this means is we
can do this ahead of time.

00:28:05.552 --> 00:28:08.010
So for example, if we're solving
an optimal control problem

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:11.280
or something like that,
we can do the permutation

00:28:11.280 --> 00:28:12.930
once and for all offline.

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:14.430
And then when this
thing is actually

00:28:14.430 --> 00:28:16.350
running a jet engine
or something like that,

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:17.850
then we don't have to do it.

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:19.740
If we're doing
finance, and we want

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:24.960
to do an insanely
fast back test,

00:28:24.960 --> 00:28:27.030
we'd first do the factorization.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:30.760
And then after that, every solve
is going to be really fast.

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:32.310
So that's how that works, yeah.

00:28:32.310 --> 00:28:33.060
So you don't know.

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:34.350
You don't have to know.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:37.650
If A is not positive
definite, and you

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:41.160
call a Cholesky factorization on
it, it will fail at some point.

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:42.660
At some point, it's
going to attempt

00:28:42.660 --> 00:28:45.210
to take the square root of
a negative number, right?

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:53.490
And then it'll throw an
exception to you of some kind.

00:28:53.490 --> 00:28:55.410
But that's the only time
you would ever know.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:56.442
Yeah?

00:28:56.442 --> 00:28:57.150
I'm just curious.

00:28:57.150 --> 00:29:00.690
When does the solver
know when to stop?

00:29:00.690 --> 00:29:02.110
What kind of criterion?

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:02.610
Oh, sorry.

00:29:02.610 --> 00:29:04.830
All of these are finite
algorithms, right?

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:07.440
And an infinite precision,
they just solve.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:08.220
Right?

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:12.450
I mean, when I say it's order n
cubed, it means I can tell you,

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:15.360
for this, you're
going to do 1 billion

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:19.560
blah, blah, blah multiplies,
1.3 billion adds.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:22.915
When it's done, OK,
if you did everything

00:29:22.915 --> 00:29:25.540
in infinite precision, you would
have the answer, end of story.

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:26.880
There's no stopping criterion.

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:29.020
There's nothing like that.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:33.420
Now, of course, you're doing
this in finite precision.

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:37.710
So since you asked, there
are methods to do something.

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.870
It's called
iterative refinement.

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:42.660
And what that does
is you compute

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:44.970
a solution that's approximate.

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:47.370
And then you can actually
do-- you can iterate

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:49.030
to get a better solution.

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:50.310
And people know about that.

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:53.040
But that's a little bit beyond
what we're talking about here.

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:54.420
But it's good to
know about, too.

00:29:54.420 --> 00:29:57.140
OK.

00:29:57.140 --> 00:30:04.470
OK so LDL transpose
factorization,

00:30:04.470 --> 00:30:08.900
this is any non-singular
symmetric matrix.

00:30:08.900 --> 00:30:11.840
By the way, we'll see examples
of where these come up maybe

00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:14.420
next week, in fact.

00:30:14.420 --> 00:30:18.950
So here, it's like an LDL
transpose factorization.

00:30:18.950 --> 00:30:22.910
But D is diagonal, and it's
either 1 by 1 or 2 by 2 blocks.

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:24.650
So that's just a
mathematical fact,

00:30:24.650 --> 00:30:26.960
that you might need to
have these 2 by 2 blocks.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:30.680
But the point is those are all
easily invertible matrices.

00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:31.410
Permutations?

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:31.910
Yes.

00:30:31.910 --> 00:30:32.750
Lower triangular?

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:33.770
Yes.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:37.220
A block diagonal matrix with
1 by 1 and 2 by 2 blocks,

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:39.230
I think we know
how to invert that.

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:41.840
That one, we actually
literally know how to invert.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:44.330
But we can certainly solve
equations like that, right?

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:51.950
So OK, so this is the LDL
transpose factorization.

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:53.810
And now we're going to
talk about something.

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:55.393
This is something
probably many of you

00:30:55.393 --> 00:30:57.290
have seen in other contexts.

00:30:57.290 --> 00:31:00.860
It comes up in pretty
much all fields.

00:31:00.860 --> 00:31:03.590
So it's something
called the Schur

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:06.510
complement, is the name of it.

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:10.442
And I think you've already
seen it early on in the class,

00:31:10.442 --> 00:31:11.400
or something like that.

00:31:11.400 --> 00:31:13.100
But I can't think of
a legitimate field

00:31:13.100 --> 00:31:14.780
where it doesn't come up.

00:31:14.780 --> 00:31:16.940
So in statistics,
it's conditioning,

00:31:16.940 --> 00:31:18.980
a Gaussian on some
entries, right?

00:31:18.980 --> 00:31:21.890
In mechanics, it's
actually what happens

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:24.110
when you impose the boundary
conditions on something

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:25.550
and ask where
everything else is.

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.530
In electrical engineering,
you take a network.

00:31:27.530 --> 00:31:30.540
And I put a termination on some
of the ports on the network.

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:32.930
And I ask, what's happening
with the rest of them?

00:31:32.930 --> 00:31:36.320
So all fields have this.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:37.100
OK.

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:39.590
And the idea is actually
embarrassingly simple.

00:31:39.590 --> 00:31:42.420
So it goes like this.

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:46.205
We're going to
write Ax equals b.

00:31:46.205 --> 00:31:49.250
We're going to block A
into some blocks, like A11.

00:31:49.250 --> 00:31:54.090
And I'm going to have A11
and A2b square, right?

00:31:54.090 --> 00:31:58.530
And I'll also conformally
block x and b.

00:31:58.530 --> 00:32:00.360
So they're going to
be called x1 stacked

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:02.550
on x2 and b1 stacked on b2.

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:03.720
Right?

00:32:03.720 --> 00:32:04.320
OK?

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:07.950
Now, OK, nothing has happened.

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:09.700
I just decided to
name it this way.

00:32:09.700 --> 00:32:11.760
So I just divided it
in some weird way.

00:32:11.760 --> 00:32:13.200
OK.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:16.800
What happens now is
you do, actually--

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:19.990
I mean, you do what you
learned in maybe high school,

00:32:19.990 --> 00:32:22.380
except those were
numbers and not matrices.

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:25.260
And so what you do
is we just work out.

00:32:25.260 --> 00:32:27.640
We can actually just
solve this in a weird way.

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:30.000
So the first equation,
block equation,

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:35.310
is A11x1 plus A12x2 equals b1.

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:36.000
Right?

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:37.445
And we're going to assume here.

00:32:37.445 --> 00:32:38.820
We're going to
make an assumption

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:43.020
that A11 is invertible, OK?

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:47.010
So what that says is,
well, I can do this.

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:50.250
This is minus A12x2, right?

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:52.860
That's that, right?

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:57.670
And then I'll just put
an A11 inverse here.

00:32:57.670 --> 00:32:58.170
OK?

00:32:58.170 --> 00:33:01.500
So there we go, like that.

00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:03.300
OK.

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.385
Yeah, by the way, already
when you see this,

00:33:05.385 --> 00:33:07.650
this is hinting that this
is going to be interesting

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:10.238
when A11 is easy to invert.

00:33:10.238 --> 00:33:12.030
So that's going to be
the interesting part.

00:33:12.030 --> 00:33:14.040
OK, but we'll get there.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:16.680
OK, I plug this into
the second equation.

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:25.770
And the second equation is
A21x1 plus A22x2 equals b2.

00:33:25.770 --> 00:33:29.610
And I plug this
expression in here.

00:33:29.610 --> 00:33:36.540
And I'm going to get something
like A21A11 inverse times b1

00:33:36.540 --> 00:33:43.440
minus A12x2 plus
A22x2 equals b2.

00:33:43.440 --> 00:33:43.950
Right?

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:46.060
So make sense.

00:33:46.060 --> 00:33:46.560
OK.

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:51.690
And then I just I rearrange
that to look like this equation

00:33:51.690 --> 00:33:53.800
here.

00:33:53.800 --> 00:33:57.030
And that's another
equation in x2.

00:33:57.030 --> 00:33:58.710
This matrix here is famous.

00:33:58.710 --> 00:34:00.600
And it's called the
Schur complement

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:02.880
of the original matrix.

00:34:02.880 --> 00:34:05.860
I can't remember if it's with
respect to the 11 or 22 block,

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:07.030
but it's one of those two.

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:08.020
And I don't remember
which it is.

00:34:08.020 --> 00:34:08.770
It doesn't matter.

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:10.090
It's a Schur complement.

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:11.290
OK?

00:34:11.290 --> 00:34:14.650
And you've probably seen it
before or something in some--

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:16.389
yeah, anyway, that's It.

00:34:16.389 --> 00:34:17.889
OK.

00:34:17.889 --> 00:34:19.480
OK.

00:34:19.480 --> 00:34:24.159
OK, so you could use
these calculations

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:25.480
to solve the original thing.

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:28.400
And that generic
algorithm looks like this.

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:31.389
It says, first, you should
form A11 inverse A12,

00:34:31.389 --> 00:34:35.570
I mean, because that's this
thing, and A11 inverse b1.

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:36.070
Right?

00:34:36.070 --> 00:34:40.639
Oh, by the way, let's talk
about how we would do that.

00:34:40.639 --> 00:34:45.699
So the way you would do that
is you would factor A11, OK?

00:34:45.699 --> 00:34:50.290
To compute this, you
would do a solve, right?

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:52.870
Because you've factored
it, and now you do a solve.

00:34:52.870 --> 00:34:57.310
To calculate this, you do a
solve on each column of A12.

00:34:57.310 --> 00:34:59.170
Everybody got that?

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:02.890
So that could end
up being quite--

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:04.450
that's quite efficient, right?

00:35:04.450 --> 00:35:09.900
So if I were to double-click
on that and expand it,

00:35:09.900 --> 00:35:10.900
it would look like this.

00:35:10.900 --> 00:35:16.360
Factor A11, then solve
with b1, and solve

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:19.600
with each column of A12, right?

00:35:19.600 --> 00:35:21.820
That gives me
these two matrices.

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:22.780
OK?

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:26.080
Next step is I form S. That's
the Schur complement, which

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:27.880
is this thing.

00:35:27.880 --> 00:35:31.250
And I form this b
tilde thing over here,

00:35:31.250 --> 00:35:33.760
which is the right-hand side
of this equation, right?

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:37.210
So this equation up here
is Sx2 equals b tilde.

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:41.200
And then what I do is I find x--

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:46.040
here I do a factor on
S, then a back solve.

00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:47.570
And then I can reassemble.

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:51.390
This gives me x2 directly, and
I can get x1 from this equation

00:35:51.390 --> 00:35:51.890
here.

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:54.290
And by the way, when
I solve this equation,

00:35:54.290 --> 00:35:56.720
I already factored A11.

00:35:56.720 --> 00:35:59.240
This is a solve, OK?

00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:00.590
Right?

00:36:00.590 --> 00:36:03.470
OK, so that's the idea.

00:36:03.470 --> 00:36:07.040
OK, now's a drum roll to
find out, is this faster?

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:07.950
Is it better?

00:36:07.950 --> 00:36:12.450
And it's actually interesting.

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:17.220
So in step one,
you have f is going

00:36:17.220 --> 00:36:19.920
to be the factor cost for A11.

00:36:19.920 --> 00:36:21.760
S is the solve cost.

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:26.100
So in the first case,
you factor A11 once,

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.520
and then you solve for
all the columns of A12.

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.860
And so that's n2S.

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:33.810
So that's the cost of step one.

00:36:33.810 --> 00:36:39.380
Step two-- and here, that's
actually just forming

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:42.162
the Schur complement matrix.

00:36:42.162 --> 00:36:43.620
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:45.968
going to imagine that
the Schur-- we're not

00:36:45.968 --> 00:36:47.760
going to exploit any
structure in the Schur

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:49.140
complement, although one could.

00:36:49.140 --> 00:36:50.098
But we're not going to.

00:36:50.098 --> 00:36:51.870
We're just going to
treat it as dense.

00:36:51.870 --> 00:36:57.000
And then step three is going to
be n2 cubed because that's just

00:36:57.000 --> 00:36:58.380
to factor and solve it.

00:36:58.380 --> 00:36:58.920
OK?

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.080
And so the total is
this number up here.

00:37:01.080 --> 00:37:03.200
That's f plus n2S
plus blah, blah, blah.

00:37:03.200 --> 00:37:04.110
OK.

00:37:04.110 --> 00:37:07.425
All right, so let's just
throw in a general A11

00:37:07.425 --> 00:37:08.970
and see what happens.

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:12.540
When you do, I mean, hardly
surprising, but actually,

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:14.670
down to the flop,
it's identical.

00:37:14.670 --> 00:37:17.250
And congratulations,
you've done a bunch

00:37:17.250 --> 00:37:18.720
of algebra and analysis.

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:20.910
And it did absolutely nothing.

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:23.340
OK?

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:26.760
Oh, by the way, it does
nothing in terms of flop count.

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:29.040
In reality, it does do things.

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:32.380
So in fact, people use these
block, this block elimination

00:37:32.380 --> 00:37:32.880
method.

00:37:32.880 --> 00:37:35.130
They actually use these
for dense matrices.

00:37:35.130 --> 00:37:38.160
And the reason is you
would choose n1 and n2,

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:39.630
so that one of
these solve methods

00:37:39.630 --> 00:37:44.070
could be done at some level
of cash and therefore, done

00:37:44.070 --> 00:37:45.690
quickly.

00:37:45.690 --> 00:37:48.120
So that actually happens,
but, again, all of us

00:37:48.120 --> 00:37:50.620
are blissfully unaware
of those details.

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:54.120
So OK, now it gets interesting.

00:37:54.120 --> 00:37:57.060
It's interesting
precisely when f is small

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.230
compared to n1 cubed.

00:37:59.230 --> 00:38:00.780
So I'm going to
give you-- here's

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:04.210
a great example is diagonal.

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:07.830
So the factor cost
for a diagonal is 0,

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:10.170
I mean, whatever, or n,
if you want to, whatever.

00:38:10.170 --> 00:38:12.030
It doesn't matter.

00:38:12.030 --> 00:38:14.442
L is going to be
the matrix itself,

00:38:14.442 --> 00:38:15.900
and you will be
the identity there.

00:38:15.900 --> 00:38:16.450
It's 0.

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:16.950
OK?

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:23.030
So here, it's actually
really interesting.

00:38:23.030 --> 00:38:27.230
You get 2n2 squared
n1 plus 2/3 n.

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:29.780
What's amazing about that
is that's linear in n1.

00:38:29.780 --> 00:38:31.790
So now I'm going to
show you some equation.

00:38:31.790 --> 00:38:35.940
I'll show you some
sparsity patterns.

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:37.328
And here it is, one.

00:38:37.328 --> 00:38:38.870
Actually, I was just
showing you one.

00:38:38.870 --> 00:38:40.920
I had one right there.

00:38:40.920 --> 00:38:42.020
Here's one.

00:38:42.020 --> 00:38:42.965
Remember this one?

00:38:47.080 --> 00:38:50.290
That's this downward
arrow pattern.

00:38:50.290 --> 00:38:53.620
Someone suggests
a blocking of it.

00:38:53.620 --> 00:38:54.970
What would be A11?

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:59.890
Do you see a leading matrix
here that is easy to invert?

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:00.520
Diagonal?

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:03.340
Yeah, yeah, so here's A11.

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:06.150
It looks like this, right?

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:07.710
It just looks like that, right?

00:39:07.710 --> 00:39:11.220
So this is A11, diagonal.

00:39:11.220 --> 00:39:13.770
Actually, I was going
to say, matrices

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:15.810
don't come easier to
invert than diagonal.

00:39:15.810 --> 00:39:16.720
That's false.

00:39:16.720 --> 00:39:21.290
Here's one that's easier, is
a multiple of the identity.

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:22.610
That's pretty straightforward.

00:39:22.610 --> 00:39:24.110
Actually, I think
in terms of flops,

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:26.110
it's the same anyway, so
it doesn't even matter.

00:39:26.110 --> 00:39:27.770
But anyway, here it is.

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:32.720
OK, so here's one where
instead of this-- so n1 is,

00:39:32.720 --> 00:39:33.290
I don't know.

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:34.915
Let's suppose the
original thing was n.

00:39:34.915 --> 00:39:36.020
It's n minus 1.

00:39:36.020 --> 00:39:42.000
And n2 is 1 because this
thing is 1 by 1 down here.

00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:44.090
And so in this
case, it turns out

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:48.380
you're going to get a stunning
reduction because you're

00:39:48.380 --> 00:39:50.810
you can invert this fast.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:53.630
And in that case, you would get
exactly this number over here.

00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:56.840
You would get 2n2
squared n1, right?

00:39:56.840 --> 00:40:02.360
So OK, that's one example,
but I'll show you some others.

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:08.570
What it means is, what if you
saw a sparsity pattern that

00:40:08.570 --> 00:40:11.270
looked like this?

00:40:11.270 --> 00:40:14.540
This is a banded.

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:18.590
And then over here,
these are dense.

00:40:18.590 --> 00:40:20.510
This is called dense
rows and columns.

00:40:20.510 --> 00:40:21.560
Everybody got that?

00:40:21.560 --> 00:40:24.710
So actually, it's just
a block arrow matrix,

00:40:24.710 --> 00:40:27.920
but with a thicker
weight on the things.

00:40:27.920 --> 00:40:29.270
Because they're wide, right?

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:32.780
That's banded, and then
this is a whole chunk

00:40:32.780 --> 00:40:33.980
of stuff over here.

00:40:33.980 --> 00:40:36.090
Everybody got that?

00:40:36.090 --> 00:40:39.890
So what this says is that this
block elimination method here

00:40:39.890 --> 00:40:42.260
will solve that.

00:40:42.260 --> 00:40:43.880
Well, it's actually insane.

00:40:43.880 --> 00:40:46.580
It's actually going to be
linear in the dimension.

00:40:46.580 --> 00:40:51.780
If this width, if this
doesn't grow this way,

00:40:51.780 --> 00:40:53.840
and if the bandwidth
doesn't change,

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:56.310
this gets bigger and
bigger, and we can solve it.

00:40:56.310 --> 00:40:56.810
Right?

00:40:56.810 --> 00:41:01.190
So anyway, it's
pretty cool, right?

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:05.870
And that means, if you see
that, you should smile.

00:41:05.870 --> 00:41:07.100
We'll put it that way.

00:41:07.100 --> 00:41:11.420
Or you're not going to see
that probably, because someone

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:12.800
would already have to know.

00:41:12.800 --> 00:41:14.450
But the way it's
really going to work

00:41:14.450 --> 00:41:16.638
is someone's going to be
talking about a problem.

00:41:16.638 --> 00:41:18.680
It's going to be some
weird, complicated problem.

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:19.290
Who knows?

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:20.040
It doesn't matter.

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:22.290
They're going be talking
about some complicated thing.

00:41:22.290 --> 00:41:24.050
While they're
talking, you will be

00:41:24.050 --> 00:41:26.690
forming in your mind
what the sparsity pattern

00:41:26.690 --> 00:41:29.240
of the associated
matrices look like.

00:41:29.240 --> 00:41:32.600
OK, when that image comes
to mind, you will smile,

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:38.270
and you will say, I can solve
that problem in half a second.

00:41:38.270 --> 00:41:42.170
And they said, I can't
even load it into memory.

00:41:42.170 --> 00:41:46.320
And you'd say, just
chill, and I'll show you

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:47.570
what we're going to do, right?

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:49.220
Everybody following this?

00:41:49.220 --> 00:41:52.460
So these are incredibly
useful things.

00:41:52.460 --> 00:41:55.170
And we can think
about what this is.

00:41:55.170 --> 00:42:00.040
This is, basically, a set of
equations where banded means

00:42:00.040 --> 00:42:04.900
that equation 50 only
references, let's say,

00:42:04.900 --> 00:42:07.770
variables 40 to 60.

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:08.270
OK?

00:42:08.270 --> 00:42:10.640
And that would be here.

00:42:10.640 --> 00:42:13.440
Then you say, but there are
some special variables here

00:42:13.440 --> 00:42:15.440
which affect everybody.

00:42:15.440 --> 00:42:15.980
OK?

00:42:15.980 --> 00:42:18.330
So that that's what this is.

00:42:18.330 --> 00:42:21.380
So you're going to want
to be making those--

00:42:21.380 --> 00:42:24.570
so yeah, to be fully
developed, I would say,

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:25.920
here's what you want.

00:42:25.920 --> 00:42:30.050
You want to be able to get
a feel for when applications

00:42:30.050 --> 00:42:33.083
involve matrices
that look like that.

00:42:33.083 --> 00:42:35.500
Then you're going to be happy
because you're going to know

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.210
something other people
in the room don't.

00:42:37.210 --> 00:42:39.168
Because you're going to
know that you can solve

00:42:39.168 --> 00:42:41.170
that in linear time, right?

00:42:41.170 --> 00:42:43.630
So that's why people
will say, like,

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:45.490
oh I have to find
this whole trajectory.

00:42:45.490 --> 00:42:48.640
It's 100,000 time points.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:50.622
And you're like, so?

00:42:50.622 --> 00:42:52.330
I mean, do you have
a phone, or whatever?

00:42:52.330 --> 00:42:54.470
It's just not going to be
that complicated, again,

00:42:54.470 --> 00:42:55.720
if you know what you're doing.

00:42:55.720 --> 00:42:56.650
OK.

00:42:56.650 --> 00:42:59.160
OK.

00:42:59.160 --> 00:43:03.660
OK, so now this leads us to--

00:43:03.660 --> 00:43:05.880
and this, probably
people have seen.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:06.930
It's got lots of names.

00:43:06.930 --> 00:43:08.710
It depends on where you're from.

00:43:08.710 --> 00:43:12.630
I think it's called
Sherman-Morrison, Woodbury.

00:43:12.630 --> 00:43:14.380
Sometimes it's called
the Woodberry thing.

00:43:14.380 --> 00:43:17.170
Sometimes it's called the
matrix inversion lemma.

00:43:17.170 --> 00:43:19.600
And I have a friend
who can actually

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:24.400
tell where someone learned their
linear algebra because they

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:25.390
use different terms.

00:43:25.390 --> 00:43:26.990
And they even use
different terms.

00:43:26.990 --> 00:43:29.230
I believe at Oxford and
Cambridge, just so you know.

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:32.470
So just by asking
someone what they

00:43:32.470 --> 00:43:34.593
call this and a couple
of other questions,

00:43:34.593 --> 00:43:36.760
you can figure out that
they got their PhD at Oxford

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:39.998
or Cambridge, or something, or
Harvard, or whatever, or MIT,

00:43:39.998 --> 00:43:40.540
or something.

00:43:40.540 --> 00:43:42.100
OK, so here it is.

00:43:42.100 --> 00:43:50.350
You start with A plus
BC times x equals b.

00:43:50.350 --> 00:43:56.050
And in fact, some people call
this a low-rank perturbation

00:43:56.050 --> 00:43:58.900
of A. I mean, that's not
fair, because no one said

00:43:58.900 --> 00:44:00.670
B and C are small.

00:44:00.670 --> 00:44:01.210
Right?

00:44:01.210 --> 00:44:05.110
But if B had just a
handful of columns,

00:44:05.110 --> 00:44:06.880
and C had a handful
of rows, this

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:09.370
would indeed be
what people would

00:44:09.370 --> 00:44:12.500
call low-rank update to A. OK?

00:44:12.500 --> 00:44:15.710
So we want to solve a
matrix that looks like that.

00:44:15.710 --> 00:44:18.680
And in fact, an extreme
case would be things like--

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:22.550
here's one you hear all the
time, diagonal plus low rank.

00:44:22.550 --> 00:44:27.260
There, A is diagonal, and
B and C are small, right?

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:30.410
So B has got a small
number of columns.

00:44:30.410 --> 00:44:32.270
C has got a small
number of rows.

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:33.590
That's super common.

00:44:33.590 --> 00:44:35.210
That's also
something you will, I

00:44:35.210 --> 00:44:39.110
would hope, never ever forget,
diagonal plus low rank.

00:44:39.110 --> 00:44:41.300
And it's all related.

00:44:41.300 --> 00:44:44.328
Here's how it's related.

00:44:44.328 --> 00:44:46.120
Yeah, actually, let's
just talk about this,

00:44:46.120 --> 00:44:47.530
in the case where
A was diagonal,

00:44:47.530 --> 00:44:51.910
and B and C have modest
number of columns.

00:44:51.910 --> 00:44:56.680
So in fact, here's one.

00:44:56.680 --> 00:44:59.140
So basically, all of
quantitative finance

00:44:59.140 --> 00:45:03.250
uses a risk model where the
covariance matrix is diagonal

00:45:03.250 --> 00:45:04.180
plus low rank.

00:45:04.180 --> 00:45:06.400
And it's called a factor model.

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:09.220
So there, C is B transpose.

00:45:09.220 --> 00:45:13.630
And if this were a
Gaussian, or something,

00:45:13.630 --> 00:45:15.550
if this is the covariance
of a Gaussian--

00:45:15.550 --> 00:45:16.690
if you understand what
I'm saying, that's fine.

00:45:16.690 --> 00:45:17.982
If you don't, that's also cool.

00:45:17.982 --> 00:45:19.630
But this is just
completely universal.

00:45:19.630 --> 00:45:20.200
Right?

00:45:20.200 --> 00:45:23.780
So what it would mean is it's
even got a Bayesian model.

00:45:23.780 --> 00:45:29.710
And it basically says that the
returns of all these assets

00:45:29.710 --> 00:45:31.870
is driven by,
let's say, B and C.

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:34.240
And typically, B is
about a width of 100.

00:45:34.240 --> 00:45:36.580
That would be very typical.

00:45:36.580 --> 00:45:39.790
C would have a high-- well,
C is B transpose, Right

00:45:39.790 --> 00:45:44.810
So you'd say, look, I don't know
what these 8,000 returns are

00:45:44.810 --> 00:45:51.080
doing, but they're mostly
driven by these 100 factors,

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:55.070
which are actually-- so
that's the underlying--

00:45:55.070 --> 00:45:57.200
that's what's actually
driving the returns.

00:45:57.200 --> 00:45:58.837
And then the A is diagonal.

00:45:58.837 --> 00:46:00.420
And they have a
beautiful name for it.

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:02.000
It's called idiosyncratic.

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:03.860
So that's the
idiosyncratic risk.

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:08.900
It's the amount by which
Google or whatever,

00:46:08.900 --> 00:46:12.523
Coca-Cola or whatever it is,
Chevron, goes up and down.

00:46:12.523 --> 00:46:14.690
In fact, the way they would
say it, this is dialect,

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:18.080
is you'd say, that is the part
of the variance not explained

00:46:18.080 --> 00:46:19.430
by the factors.

00:46:19.430 --> 00:46:20.540
Everyone got that?

00:46:20.540 --> 00:46:21.378
So that's it.

00:46:21.378 --> 00:46:23.420
And the factors are actually
kind of interesting.

00:46:23.420 --> 00:46:24.837
Sorry, this is
just a weird aside.

00:46:24.837 --> 00:46:26.630
But since we're here,
I'll just continue.

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:27.830
The factors are interesting.

00:46:27.830 --> 00:46:29.600
Some are handcrafted.

00:46:29.600 --> 00:46:32.627
So they're literally
matrices with 0s and 1s

00:46:32.627 --> 00:46:33.710
and weird stuff like that.

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:39.710
And what region are you in?

00:46:39.710 --> 00:46:43.820
Are you a
pharmaceuticals company?

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:46.070
Or you do defense, do you
do that kind of thing?

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:48.680
Others are actually
crafted by people

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:50.030
using numerical algorithms.

00:46:50.030 --> 00:46:53.030
So OK, that's just a
small story about this.

00:46:53.030 --> 00:46:54.840
OK.

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:57.840
Oh, if I have something that
looks like that, A is diagonal,

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:02.430
and B and C are small, I
can store that, no problem.

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:06.750
Even for very big, if you
want to run a fund that

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:08.370
is looking at a universe--

00:47:08.370 --> 00:47:10.170
I'm using dialect--
of, let's say,

00:47:10.170 --> 00:47:14.700
100,000 assets across
the whole world,

00:47:14.700 --> 00:47:15.870
I know people who do that.

00:47:15.870 --> 00:47:17.740
So you do that.

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:20.580
So the covariance matrix is
100,000 by 100,000 matrix.

00:47:20.580 --> 00:47:23.160
For something like that,
someone will say, well,

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:24.480
what's your risk model?

00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:29.340
And they would say, oh, I
have a 200 factor model.

00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:31.740
And that means their
covariance matrix

00:47:31.740 --> 00:47:35.280
is expressed as a diagonal
matrix, which is whatever

00:47:35.280 --> 00:47:39.570
it is, 100,000 by 100,000.

00:47:39.570 --> 00:47:42.360
And then they have 200 factors.

00:47:42.360 --> 00:47:44.860
And all of that is completely
storable, and stuff like that.

00:47:44.860 --> 00:47:46.860
And you'll see, in fact,
not only can you store,

00:47:46.860 --> 00:47:48.750
you can actually solve
problems super fast,

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:49.625
and things like that.

00:47:49.625 --> 00:47:51.840
OK, that was all a weird aside.

00:47:51.840 --> 00:47:53.572
So let's keep going.

00:47:53.572 --> 00:47:55.780
So now what you do here is
something that is actually

00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:59.260
a little bit unintuitive.

00:47:59.260 --> 00:48:02.180
It's called you uneliminate.

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:05.810
We did this before in duality,
and it turned out well there.

00:48:05.810 --> 00:48:07.880
What you do, to
uneliminate means

00:48:07.880 --> 00:48:09.740
to introduce new variables.

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:13.125
To eliminate variables
means to solve for them,

00:48:13.125 --> 00:48:14.000
get rid of them, And.

00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:17.030
Then substitute back their
values into the rest.

00:48:17.030 --> 00:48:18.180
That's elimination.

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:18.680
Right?

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:21.890
That sounds good, especially
to an amateur or somebody

00:48:21.890 --> 00:48:23.060
first seeing this material.

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:25.620
Because what are you doing?

00:48:25.620 --> 00:48:26.870
So I'm eliminating some-- why?

00:48:26.870 --> 00:48:29.660
Because I like to solve
smaller sets of equations.

00:48:29.660 --> 00:48:33.380
I mean, everyone, like, it
makes perfect sense, right?

00:48:33.380 --> 00:48:35.600
It's completely
and totally wrong.

00:48:35.600 --> 00:48:37.790
That's not how you should
be doing linear algebra,

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:39.710
but it does sound
good, and people

00:48:39.710 --> 00:48:41.420
can fall for that, right?

00:48:41.420 --> 00:48:41.960
OK.

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:43.355
Unelimination is the opposite.

00:48:43.355 --> 00:48:45.230
You say, thank you for
your set of equations.

00:48:45.230 --> 00:48:47.540
If you don't mind, I'm going
to introduce new variables

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:49.190
and equations and
make it a bigger one.

00:48:49.190 --> 00:48:52.902
And someone's like,
why would you do that?

00:48:52.902 --> 00:48:54.110
Everyone see what I'm saying?

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:56.480
You'd say, thank
you for your 1,000

00:48:56.480 --> 00:48:57.632
by 1,000 set of equations.

00:48:57.632 --> 00:48:59.840
If you don't mind, I'm going
to expand it to a 10,000

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:02.640
by 10,000 set of equations,
if it's OK with you.

00:49:02.640 --> 00:49:05.880
And they would say, like, why?

00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:08.700
And the answer is, you'll
see why in a minute.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:09.960
So we write this this way.

00:49:09.960 --> 00:49:11.440
We just introduce
a new variable,

00:49:11.440 --> 00:49:14.220
which is y, which is Cx.

00:49:14.220 --> 00:49:17.550
And I write that second equation
as this bottom row, which

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:21.390
is Cx minus y equals 0, right?

00:49:21.390 --> 00:49:24.570
So the second system
of equations is bigger.

00:49:24.570 --> 00:49:29.220
And it is completely equivalent
to the first one, right?

00:49:29.220 --> 00:49:29.730
OK.

00:49:29.730 --> 00:49:33.450
Now let's talk about this.

00:49:33.450 --> 00:49:36.060
When you see a matrix
like that-- you just

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:39.880
encountered all this so
far just a few minutes ago,

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:41.970
so I don't expect you to
have this response now.

00:49:41.970 --> 00:49:44.670
But hopefully,
within a week or two,

00:49:44.670 --> 00:49:47.400
you should have the
following response.

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:50.100
You will see a block
there, a minus I,

00:49:50.100 --> 00:49:53.880
and your eye will go to
it because it's really

00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:55.217
easily inverted.

00:49:55.217 --> 00:49:56.550
And you're going to see a block.

00:49:56.550 --> 00:49:59.790
And you're going to
have an urge to do

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:01.800
block elimination on this.

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:03.097
Right?

00:50:03.097 --> 00:50:04.680
And so what you would
do is you'd say,

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:06.013
oh, I know what I'm going to do.

00:50:06.013 --> 00:50:08.978
I'm going to permute the I,
the minus I, up to the top.

00:50:08.978 --> 00:50:10.770
And then I'm going to
use block elimination

00:50:10.770 --> 00:50:12.600
from the previous stuff.

00:50:12.600 --> 00:50:13.620
Everybody got that?

00:50:13.620 --> 00:50:15.270
And then you eliminate it.

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:18.478
And you will end
up, then, forming

00:50:18.478 --> 00:50:19.770
the Schur complement equations.

00:50:19.770 --> 00:50:22.080
And it will be A
plus BCx equals b.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:24.490
So you'll be right
back where you started.

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:24.990
OK.

00:50:24.990 --> 00:50:26.880
So here's what you do instead.

00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:32.460
You resist the urge here
to eliminate the minus I,

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.680
even though it is a
very attractive target

00:50:34.680 --> 00:50:37.590
for elimination because
it's easily inverted, right?

00:50:37.590 --> 00:50:42.045
You resist that urge, and
instead, you eliminate A.

00:50:42.045 --> 00:50:44.280
And you will end up
with a Schur compliment

00:50:44.280 --> 00:50:47.210
equation that looks like this.

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:48.650
OK.

00:50:48.650 --> 00:50:51.440
And this allows you to get y.

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:52.700
Here, you solve for y.

00:50:52.700 --> 00:50:55.370
Then from that, you plug
it back in and get x.

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:57.223
And you end up with
a formula that's

00:50:57.223 --> 00:50:58.640
called the matrix
inversion lemma.

00:50:58.640 --> 00:50:59.930
That's the neutral version.

00:50:59.930 --> 00:51:04.340
It says that A plus BC inverse
is this horrible formula

00:51:04.340 --> 00:51:05.220
over here.

00:51:05.220 --> 00:51:07.610
Yeah, so that's it.

00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:11.330
This gets interesting
if A is diagonal,

00:51:11.330 --> 00:51:14.120
or something like that.

00:51:14.120 --> 00:51:17.660
Anyway, a lot of things
would come out of that.

00:51:17.660 --> 00:51:21.200
How many people have seen matrix
inversion lemma or something

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:24.680
before, or some
Sherman-Morrison-Woodbury?

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:25.670
It's the same.

00:51:25.670 --> 00:51:27.470
OK.

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:29.150
Any other names for it?

00:51:29.150 --> 00:51:31.640
I used to know at least
one or two others.

00:51:31.640 --> 00:51:36.348
But anyway, anybody else
know another name for that?

00:51:36.348 --> 00:51:37.640
It's a thing in linear algebra.

00:51:37.640 --> 00:51:40.310
And how you call it depends
on where you came from.

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:45.550
So OK, OK, so I think,
actually, I'll--

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:47.020
oh yeah, well,
here's an example.

00:51:47.020 --> 00:51:50.080
A diagonal and B
and C are dense.

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:59.050
This is basically, you're
going to form D is A plus BC.

00:51:59.050 --> 00:52:00.880
This is if you do it
the dumb way, right?

00:52:00.880 --> 00:52:04.840
You just write it out as a
dense matrix, then solve.

00:52:04.840 --> 00:52:08.440
In this case, then you get
something that's Pn squared--

00:52:08.440 --> 00:52:09.250
sorry, n cubed.

00:52:09.250 --> 00:52:10.750
And if you did it
the right way, you

00:52:10.750 --> 00:52:12.910
get something
that's linear in n.

00:52:12.910 --> 00:52:14.830
And these make a big difference.

00:52:17.630 --> 00:52:19.370
But by the way, a
lot of this stuff

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:23.900
subsumes things that were taught
in the '60s and '70s and '80s

00:52:23.900 --> 00:52:26.062
and sometimes, in
the '90s by people

00:52:26.062 --> 00:52:27.770
who learned it from
people who learned it

00:52:27.770 --> 00:52:28.780
in the '60s and '70s.

00:52:28.780 --> 00:52:32.960
OK, so for example, if you
teach a course on control,

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:34.190
you might--

00:52:34.190 --> 00:52:34.903
I've done this.

00:52:34.903 --> 00:52:36.320
I haven't done it
for a long time.

00:52:36.320 --> 00:52:37.280
I won't do it again.

00:52:37.280 --> 00:52:39.080
But you go through
a long derivation

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:42.710
of what the Kalman filter is or
how to solve an optimal control

00:52:42.710 --> 00:52:43.513
problem, right?

00:52:43.513 --> 00:52:44.930
And it's very
clever, and you have

00:52:44.930 --> 00:52:47.430
little parts and this Bellman
equation and blah, blah, blah.

00:52:47.430 --> 00:52:50.400
And yeah, you end up with
stuff that actually works.

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:52.310
That's great.

00:52:52.310 --> 00:52:53.840
A very funny thing happens.

00:52:53.840 --> 00:52:56.780
If you simply take the
problem represented

00:52:56.780 --> 00:53:00.050
by an optimal control
problem or a Kalman filter

00:53:00.050 --> 00:53:03.740
and just throw it at a
sparse solver, guess what?

00:53:03.740 --> 00:53:06.722
It'll be just as-- it'll do it.

00:53:06.722 --> 00:53:08.180
So basically, all
of those lectures

00:53:08.180 --> 00:53:09.555
were useless, is
what I'm saying,

00:53:09.555 --> 00:53:11.763
as a practical matter, which
is kind of embarrassing,

00:53:11.763 --> 00:53:12.470
but whatever.

00:53:12.470 --> 00:53:13.988
So I don't teach that anymore.

00:53:13.988 --> 00:53:15.530
So I don't teach it
that way anymore.

00:53:15.530 --> 00:53:19.380
I just say, you get a giant
set of linear equations,

00:53:19.380 --> 00:53:24.060
and you solve it
using sparse methods.

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:24.810
Done.

00:53:24.810 --> 00:53:26.800
That is the correct
way to do it.

00:53:26.800 --> 00:53:28.680
It gives you a lot
of stuff, right?

00:53:28.680 --> 00:53:32.970
So this is in a lot of fields.

00:53:32.970 --> 00:53:34.650
I'm almost certain,
in statistics,

00:53:34.650 --> 00:53:36.630
there would be many,
many papers written

00:53:36.630 --> 00:53:38.710
on if you had a factor model.

00:53:38.710 --> 00:53:41.400
So that's a covariance matrix
is diagonal plus low rank.

00:53:41.400 --> 00:53:44.610
And it would be all sorts of
things about very intensely

00:53:44.610 --> 00:53:46.290
clever ways I could condition.

00:53:46.290 --> 00:53:48.850
Suppose I give you 80% of
the entries of a vector,

00:53:48.850 --> 00:53:50.850
and I said, please compute
the conditional means

00:53:50.850 --> 00:53:52.020
of the other remaining ones?

00:53:52.020 --> 00:53:53.160
Everybody follow this?

00:53:53.160 --> 00:53:55.650
That's a conditional
mean imputation task.

00:53:55.650 --> 00:53:56.250
Right?

00:53:56.250 --> 00:53:58.800
So I'm sure there's
whole papers written

00:53:58.800 --> 00:54:01.780
on this and clever
methods for doing this.

00:54:01.780 --> 00:54:05.700
And anyway, the answer is you
form the covariance matrix,

00:54:05.700 --> 00:54:09.070
literally write down the
equations you want to solve.

00:54:09.070 --> 00:54:13.230
And then you do nothing but
pass it to a sparse solver.

00:54:13.230 --> 00:54:18.060
And it'll be just as fancy,
probably more general

00:54:18.060 --> 00:54:19.330
than all those papers.

00:54:19.330 --> 00:54:21.990
So OK, it's
embarrassing, but true.

00:54:21.990 --> 00:54:23.100
OK.

00:54:23.100 --> 00:54:25.130
All right.

00:54:25.130 --> 00:54:27.570
So I'm not going
to go into this.

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:29.420
There's a whole lot
more I haven't told you

00:54:29.420 --> 00:54:30.680
about linear algebra.

00:54:30.680 --> 00:54:32.760
But these are the main points.

00:54:35.990 --> 00:54:37.755
And right now, this
is just hanging here.

00:54:37.755 --> 00:54:39.380
Now you just know
about linear algebra.

00:54:39.380 --> 00:54:42.500
But I promise in
the next week, we're

00:54:42.500 --> 00:54:47.450
going to connect it to problems
and to optimization problems.

00:54:47.450 --> 00:54:49.910
And that will have a
connection to applications.

00:54:49.910 --> 00:54:52.640
And then you will start
being able to listen

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:56.030
to a problem, a practical
problem, and say,

00:54:56.030 --> 00:54:57.470
I can solve that.

00:54:57.470 --> 00:54:59.690
I can solve that super fast.

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:02.420
So everybody getting this?

00:55:02.420 --> 00:55:05.180
So I mean, there's people
where this is all they do.

00:55:05.180 --> 00:55:06.680
If you do scientific
computation,

00:55:06.680 --> 00:55:08.990
this is a lot of
the stuff you do.

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:09.590
OK.

00:55:09.590 --> 00:55:15.350
So now we're going to start
unconstrained minimization.

00:55:15.350 --> 00:55:19.460
And like I say, we're building
slowly up at the lowest level.

00:55:19.460 --> 00:55:23.030
Well, at the lowest
level are computers,

00:55:23.030 --> 00:55:25.350
compilers, microcode,
things like that, which

00:55:25.350 --> 00:55:28.470
we're not going to look at.

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:31.450
The bottom floor is what we just
covered, this linear algebra.

00:55:31.450 --> 00:55:35.050
Now, the next floor
is, how do you solve

00:55:35.050 --> 00:55:36.620
smooth, unconstrained problems?

00:55:36.620 --> 00:55:43.060
So we'll look at that first,
and we'll build slowly up.

00:55:43.060 --> 00:55:44.620
Then we'll get
equality constraints.

00:55:44.620 --> 00:55:46.370
Then we'll pull in
inequality constraints.

00:55:46.370 --> 00:55:47.680
And then we're there.

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:50.410
So OK, so here's
what it looks like.

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:53.560
We're going to minimize
a function f here

00:55:53.560 --> 00:55:55.180
that is-- we're
going to assume it's

00:55:55.180 --> 00:55:59.620
smooth and twice continuously
differentiable, right?

00:55:59.620 --> 00:56:05.375
And we'll assume that is the
optimal value is achieved,

00:56:05.375 --> 00:56:07.000
so there's, at least,
an optimal point.

00:56:07.000 --> 00:56:07.500
Right?

00:56:07.500 --> 00:56:08.875
Now by the way,
a lot of problems

00:56:08.875 --> 00:56:10.840
like that that we want
to solve in this class,

00:56:10.840 --> 00:56:14.950
problems you have already solved
don't satisfy this, right?

00:56:14.950 --> 00:56:17.680
If you did an L1
fitting problem,

00:56:17.680 --> 00:56:20.032
you would minimize the
L1 norm of Ax minus b.

00:56:20.032 --> 00:56:20.990
And that doesn't count.

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:23.500
That's not here, because
that's not differentiable.

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:25.030
OK.

00:56:25.030 --> 00:56:28.180
OK, so this is what
we're going to do.

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:31.910
In general, except for a very
important set of problems

00:56:31.910 --> 00:56:34.970
we will see shortly, there
are no analytical solutions

00:56:34.970 --> 00:56:35.480
to this.

00:56:38.060 --> 00:56:41.390
So instead, we're going
to have iterative methods.

00:56:41.390 --> 00:56:45.290
And an iterative method
is going to generate an x.

00:56:45.290 --> 00:56:47.630
It's going to query
something about f

00:56:47.630 --> 00:56:50.282
and then generate a new x, and
so on, a new x and a new x.

00:56:50.282 --> 00:56:51.740
And what's going
to happen is those

00:56:51.740 --> 00:56:56.360
x's are going to converge
to, hopefully, a solution

00:56:56.360 --> 00:56:58.790
of this problem.

00:56:58.790 --> 00:57:01.820
And what we'll do is--

00:57:01.820 --> 00:57:03.770
the typical thing
you're going to get

00:57:03.770 --> 00:57:05.860
is something like
this sequence is

00:57:05.860 --> 00:57:07.610
going to have the
function values converge

00:57:07.610 --> 00:57:09.480
to the optimal value.

00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:12.682
And another way to say
it is that the gradient,

00:57:12.682 --> 00:57:14.390
you're going to have
a sequence of points

00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:16.610
where the gradient gets
smaller and smaller.

00:57:16.610 --> 00:57:17.390
OK?

00:57:17.390 --> 00:57:19.940
And then when you deem
it to be small enough--

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:21.920
you asked earlier about
stopping criteria.

00:57:21.920 --> 00:57:23.480
Here you have stopping criteria.

00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:26.420
For the direct methods, you
don't have stopping criteria.

00:57:26.420 --> 00:57:27.860
Well, it just stops.

00:57:27.860 --> 00:57:30.620
It's like after n
cubed over 3 steps,

00:57:30.620 --> 00:57:32.030
you've got the Cholesky factor.

00:57:32.030 --> 00:57:33.710
There is no stopping criterion.

00:57:33.710 --> 00:57:35.460
Here there is.

00:57:35.460 --> 00:57:36.980
OK.

00:57:36.980 --> 00:57:40.018
So we'll make some assumptions.

00:57:40.018 --> 00:57:41.810
I don't want to get
too technical about it,

00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:45.807
because it quickly gets
boring, and stuff like that.

00:57:45.807 --> 00:57:47.390
And by the way,
there's entire classes

00:57:47.390 --> 00:57:50.900
that cover nothing but this
material in hideous, full

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:52.350
mathematical detail.

00:57:52.350 --> 00:57:54.020
So I mean, they're interesting.

00:57:54.020 --> 00:57:56.900
But I don't know.

00:57:56.900 --> 00:58:00.180
I think some people
grow out of it.

00:58:00.180 --> 00:58:01.880
Anyway, I'll stop here.

00:58:01.880 --> 00:58:03.450
OK, that's fine.

00:58:03.450 --> 00:58:03.950
OK.

00:58:07.180 --> 00:58:11.170
So we're going to assume that
the sublevel sets are closed.

00:58:11.170 --> 00:58:13.540
And one condition for
that is pretty common,

00:58:13.540 --> 00:58:16.580
is that the function
f is closed.

00:58:16.580 --> 00:58:19.600
If you do convex analysis, which
is the mathematics of convex

00:58:19.600 --> 00:58:23.140
optimization and convexity,
this is a central concept.

00:58:23.140 --> 00:58:25.270
It just means that the
epigraph is closed.

00:58:25.270 --> 00:58:27.340
Right?

00:58:27.340 --> 00:58:31.750
And one example would
be if you had a--

00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:34.150
here are some examples
of some closed functions.

00:58:34.150 --> 00:58:36.610
Log sum x, this is
because the domain of this

00:58:36.610 --> 00:58:37.810
is anything, right?

00:58:37.810 --> 00:58:40.408
So this would count here.

00:58:40.408 --> 00:58:41.200
Here's another one.

00:58:41.200 --> 00:58:44.290
This is weird because you might
say, but there's a constraint.

00:58:44.290 --> 00:58:46.960
The constraint is that
x lies in the interior

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:48.040
of this polyhedron.

00:58:48.040 --> 00:58:50.148
Because otherwise, I mean,
there's-- so there's an

00:58:50.148 --> 00:58:51.190
embedded constraint here.

00:58:51.190 --> 00:58:52.898
And you might say,
no, I don't like that.

00:58:52.898 --> 00:58:54.490
But this one is perfectly good.

00:58:54.490 --> 00:58:55.930
All the sublevel
sets are closed.

00:58:55.930 --> 00:58:58.480
And the reason is that
the function value

00:58:58.480 --> 00:59:00.820
goes to infinity as you
approach the boundary.

00:59:00.820 --> 00:59:02.470
So we'll get to that.

00:59:02.470 --> 00:59:03.590
OK?

00:59:03.590 --> 00:59:04.090
OK.

00:59:04.090 --> 00:59:05.860
So these are
technical conditions,

00:59:05.860 --> 00:59:08.150
I think, not super,
duper important.

00:59:10.970 --> 00:59:14.330
People sometimes
assume something

00:59:14.330 --> 00:59:17.600
like a minimum curvature, right?

00:59:17.600 --> 00:59:21.080
So what that says is, for
example, the minimum eigenvalue

00:59:21.080 --> 00:59:23.030
of the Hessian is
bigger than some number

00:59:23.030 --> 00:59:24.540
m, which is positive.

00:59:24.540 --> 00:59:25.040
Right?

00:59:25.040 --> 00:59:28.430
And that gives you a minimum
curvature to the function.

00:59:28.430 --> 00:59:32.780
That will allow you to
say all sorts of things.

00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:38.120
Yeah, now, you can ask questions
like, how would you know

00:59:38.120 --> 00:59:39.920
what the minimum curvature is?

00:59:39.920 --> 00:59:41.480
It's a good question, right?

00:59:41.480 --> 00:59:42.870
And the answer
is, in most cases,

00:59:42.870 --> 00:59:44.287
you would have
absolutely no idea.

00:59:44.287 --> 00:59:46.130
There are cases where you do.

00:59:46.130 --> 00:59:48.530
Oh, here's one.

00:59:48.530 --> 00:59:51.230
Let's say I do multinomial
logistic regression,

00:59:51.230 --> 00:59:55.160
and I add quadratic
regularization to it.

00:59:55.160 --> 00:59:58.370
Then I don't even
count the curvature

00:59:58.370 --> 01:00:01.650
that comes from the multinomial
logistic regression loss.

01:00:01.650 --> 01:00:04.880
And just the quadratic
regularization alone

01:00:04.880 --> 01:00:06.140
gives me a minimum curvature.

01:00:06.140 --> 01:00:07.760
Everybody follow me?

01:00:07.760 --> 01:00:09.410
But these are rare.

01:00:09.410 --> 01:00:11.250
But that is an example,
where you actually

01:00:11.250 --> 01:00:16.860
would know a candidate value
for m, which is literally the--

01:00:16.860 --> 01:00:18.630
it's going to be
the regularization--

01:00:18.630 --> 01:00:21.540
the ridge regression parameter.

01:00:21.540 --> 01:00:22.110
OK.

01:00:22.110 --> 01:00:25.290
So if this is the
case, then this

01:00:25.290 --> 01:00:32.130
says that f minus m times
the norm of x squared

01:00:32.130 --> 01:00:34.890
is actually convex.

01:00:34.890 --> 01:00:38.310
And if you apply that, if you
then apply the basic convexity

01:00:38.310 --> 01:00:41.350
formula to that, you'll
find out a really cool--

01:00:41.350 --> 01:00:43.330
you get a strengthened--

01:00:43.330 --> 01:00:45.450
I guess it's not
Jensen, whatever.

01:00:45.450 --> 01:00:48.540
This first part, up
to here, that's true.

01:00:48.540 --> 01:00:53.010
This is true for any
convex function, this part.

01:00:53.010 --> 01:00:54.360
That's the basic inequality.

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:56.130
What this says is, oh yeah?

01:00:56.130 --> 01:00:58.950
Well, it applies with
a little bit more.

01:00:58.950 --> 01:01:01.530
That's the minimum
curvature constraint, right?

01:01:01.530 --> 01:01:03.990
So it strengthens
that basic inequality

01:01:03.990 --> 01:01:06.360
we saw six weeks ago,
or whenever it was,

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:10.300
seven weeks, ago, six, right?

01:01:10.300 --> 01:01:10.800
OK.

01:01:10.800 --> 01:01:12.570
And this means all
sorts of cool things.

01:01:12.570 --> 01:01:16.050
Because for example,
I could minimize

01:01:16.050 --> 01:01:19.110
both of these equations over y.

01:01:19.110 --> 01:01:22.350
On the left, I get P
star because it's just

01:01:22.350 --> 01:01:23.880
the minimum of f of y.

01:01:23.880 --> 01:01:27.540
On the right, this is a
quadratic function in y.

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:28.950
I set the gradient equal to 0.

01:01:28.950 --> 01:01:31.890
I minimize it, and you get
a number which is actually--

01:01:31.890 --> 01:01:33.210
it's going to be--

01:01:33.210 --> 01:01:35.570
anyway, you end up with
something that looks like this.

01:01:35.570 --> 01:01:37.390
You end up with this
inequality, right?

01:01:37.390 --> 01:01:40.840
And so this is cool
because this says

01:01:40.840 --> 01:01:43.960
that, if you wanted
a stopping criterion,

01:01:43.960 --> 01:01:45.370
you would run the algorithm.

01:01:45.370 --> 01:01:46.550
You'd evaluate the gradient.

01:01:46.550 --> 01:01:48.633
And when the gradient gets
small enough, you quit.

01:01:48.633 --> 01:01:51.165
And someone says,
why are you quitting?

01:01:51.165 --> 01:01:52.540
And you'd say,
well, the gradient

01:01:52.540 --> 01:01:53.290
is less than something.

01:01:53.290 --> 01:01:55.390
And they go, yeah, but
how far am I from optimal?

01:01:55.390 --> 01:01:57.310
And then you would
say, oh, good question.

01:01:57.310 --> 01:01:58.480
I can, actually bound it.

01:01:58.480 --> 01:01:59.950
I can say that
here's how far you

01:01:59.950 --> 01:02:03.240
are from the optimal value, no
more than 1 over 2m times this.

01:02:03.240 --> 01:02:04.740
Of course, then,
in general, someone

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:06.160
would say, how do you know m?

01:02:06.160 --> 01:02:08.285
And the answer is, you
don't, and blah, blah, blah.

01:02:08.285 --> 01:02:13.300
So OK, but this is
the justification

01:02:13.300 --> 01:02:16.960
you would have for stopping
when the gradient is small.

01:02:16.960 --> 01:02:19.750
OK.

01:02:19.750 --> 01:02:22.240
So we'll say a little bit
about descent methods.

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:27.610
These are super widely used.

01:02:27.610 --> 01:02:30.820
And they all have--
there's a generic form.

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:32.980
And it looks like this.

01:02:32.980 --> 01:02:36.880
In each step of
a descent method,

01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:38.950
you're going to find
something called delta x.

01:02:38.950 --> 01:02:43.820
And that's called the step or
the search direction, here.

01:02:43.820 --> 01:02:47.590
And so basically, what
happens is, at iteration k,

01:02:47.590 --> 01:02:48.700
you have xk.

01:02:48.700 --> 01:02:53.200
You make some queries to f
about what's the gradient,

01:02:53.200 --> 01:02:54.280
or something like that.

01:02:54.280 --> 01:02:56.333
And then you form
this search direction.

01:02:56.333 --> 01:02:58.000
So you stop, and you
ask for directions.

01:02:58.000 --> 01:02:59.710
And it says, well, you're here.

01:02:59.710 --> 01:03:00.722
You want to minimize.

01:03:00.722 --> 01:03:01.930
You should go that direction.

01:03:01.930 --> 01:03:02.830
That's delta x.

01:03:02.830 --> 01:03:04.305
That's this direction.

01:03:04.305 --> 01:03:06.680
Then what you do is you're
going to go in that direction,

01:03:06.680 --> 01:03:09.760
but an amount that you're
going to then choose.

01:03:09.760 --> 01:03:11.680
And that amount is
scaled by this factor

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:13.210
t, which is positive.

01:03:13.210 --> 01:03:18.320
And that's called, sometimes,
the step size or the step

01:03:18.320 --> 01:03:20.360
length, even though
it need not be--

01:03:20.360 --> 01:03:23.210
it's not really the length
unless the norm of the gradient

01:03:23.210 --> 01:03:24.500
is equal to 1, right?

01:03:24.500 --> 01:03:27.050
But still, people call it the
step length or the step size

01:03:27.050 --> 01:03:28.320
or something like that.

01:03:28.320 --> 01:03:32.450
So OK, that's what you do.

01:03:32.450 --> 01:03:37.970
And it turns out, if the
direction you move in

01:03:37.970 --> 01:03:41.180
has a negative inner
product with the gradient,

01:03:41.180 --> 01:03:43.700
then it turns out--

01:03:43.700 --> 01:03:46.610
and if you choose the
step size correctly,

01:03:46.610 --> 01:03:49.062
the new point will actually
have a function value

01:03:49.062 --> 01:03:50.270
less than the original point.

01:03:50.270 --> 01:03:50.770
That's good.

01:03:50.770 --> 01:03:54.470
That's called a descent method
because you're descending,

01:03:54.470 --> 01:03:57.240
and you end up with a
function value that's lower.

01:03:57.240 --> 01:03:57.740
OK?

01:03:57.740 --> 01:04:01.280
So here's a generic
version of it.

01:04:01.280 --> 01:04:05.180
To make this a real algorithm,
we have to fill in things.

01:04:05.180 --> 01:04:08.530
We have to figure out, how do
you find the descent direction?

01:04:08.530 --> 01:04:10.150
And then we have
to figure out, how

01:04:10.150 --> 01:04:11.560
do you choose the step length?

01:04:11.560 --> 01:04:12.190
Right?

01:04:12.190 --> 01:04:13.717
And that's the
whole thing, right?

01:04:13.717 --> 01:04:16.300
And the stopping criterion could
be some norm of the gradient.

01:04:16.300 --> 01:04:17.560
Usually, it's not.

01:04:17.560 --> 01:04:19.900
Usually, it's something
that is computed

01:04:19.900 --> 01:04:22.300
as a byproduct of these
two steps in here.

01:04:22.300 --> 01:04:25.300
So this is going to be
hundreds of different methods

01:04:25.300 --> 01:04:29.260
with different names are going
to have exactly this form.

01:04:29.260 --> 01:04:32.600
And they differ in, what's
the search direction?

01:04:32.600 --> 01:04:35.330
And they differ in,
what is the line search?

01:04:35.330 --> 01:04:35.830
OK?

01:04:35.830 --> 01:04:40.070
So that's the idea.

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:44.600
So for line searches,
there's a bunch.

01:04:44.600 --> 01:04:45.575
I mean, here's one.

01:04:45.575 --> 01:04:47.180
One is an exact line search.

01:04:47.180 --> 01:04:49.760
That says, once you've
decided that you're here,

01:04:49.760 --> 01:04:51.440
and you're going
in that direction,

01:04:51.440 --> 01:04:54.020
you actually look
at the function

01:04:54.020 --> 01:04:55.940
restricted to that ray.

01:04:55.940 --> 01:04:59.390
And then you look for the
point where along that ray,

01:04:59.390 --> 01:05:00.710
you have the smallest value.

01:05:00.710 --> 01:05:02.870
That's called an
exact line search.

01:05:02.870 --> 01:05:05.020
And you can do
this in some cases

01:05:05.020 --> 01:05:07.730
where you can actually
work this out analytically,

01:05:07.730 --> 01:05:10.430
or you can compute it cheaply,
or something like that.

01:05:10.430 --> 01:05:15.470
So that's one place where
you might do this, right?

01:05:15.470 --> 01:05:18.350
Another one, which
sounds much cruder,

01:05:18.350 --> 01:05:20.120
is called a backtracking
line search.

01:05:20.120 --> 01:05:21.690
And this has many,
many names, again,

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:23.065
depending on where
you came from.

01:05:23.065 --> 01:05:25.932
It's called an
Armijo line search.

01:05:25.932 --> 01:05:27.390
It's got all sorts
of names for it.

01:05:27.390 --> 01:05:29.570
An Armijo condition is
at least one of those.

01:05:29.570 --> 01:05:30.890
I forget what it is
in Russian, but they

01:05:30.890 --> 01:05:32.098
have a different name for it.

01:05:32.098 --> 01:05:35.987
So there are lots of
different names for this.

01:05:35.987 --> 01:05:37.820
So this has been
rediscovered, I don't know,

01:05:37.820 --> 01:05:39.290
a couple hundred times.

01:05:39.290 --> 01:05:42.240
Here's the way it works.

01:05:42.240 --> 01:05:43.410
It's called backtracking.

01:05:43.410 --> 01:05:44.410
And it goes like this.

01:05:44.410 --> 01:05:47.370
You start with t equals 1.

01:05:47.370 --> 01:05:51.180
So you try x plus delta x.

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:52.890
Then what you're
going to do is you're

01:05:52.890 --> 01:05:55.540
going to check whether
f has gone down--

01:05:55.540 --> 01:05:57.720
and this is critical-- enough.

01:05:57.720 --> 01:06:01.830
By the way, if it
went up, that's,

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:03.870
basically, that's
really bad, right?

01:06:03.870 --> 01:06:05.610
In fact, you can even
be out of domain.

01:06:05.610 --> 01:06:09.150
So that would be it went
up a lot because it went up

01:06:09.150 --> 01:06:09.880
to plus infinity.

01:06:09.880 --> 01:06:10.380
Right?

01:06:10.380 --> 01:06:13.330
So OK, so here's
what this looks like.

01:06:13.330 --> 01:06:17.190
Here's t here.

01:06:17.190 --> 01:06:21.210
Here is the function
restricted to t.

01:06:21.210 --> 01:06:25.200
This would be the exact
line search number.

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:27.210
That's the exact step
length you would take.

01:06:27.210 --> 01:06:29.850
You would take this to
minimize this, right?

01:06:29.850 --> 01:06:34.060
So the way this works is you
look at this basic inequality

01:06:34.060 --> 01:06:34.560
here.

01:06:34.560 --> 01:06:37.290
That basic inequality
is this line right here,

01:06:37.290 --> 01:06:39.120
this dashed line.

01:06:39.120 --> 01:06:42.180
And what it says
is, if I put alpha

01:06:42.180 --> 01:06:45.960
equals 1 in that equation, I
can reverse that inequality.

01:06:45.960 --> 01:06:49.770
Because basically, when I
continue this linear thing,

01:06:49.770 --> 01:06:51.540
this is a lower bound on this.

01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:52.200
Right?

01:06:52.200 --> 01:06:55.260
So the actual function
value could never

01:06:55.260 --> 01:06:58.500
be below this if the
function is convex, right?

01:06:58.500 --> 01:07:00.150
So here's what you do.

01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:05.263
We put an alpha in there, which
is a number that's less than 1.

01:07:05.263 --> 01:07:07.680
Actually, in fact, it's less
than 1/2 , for weird reasons.

01:07:07.680 --> 01:07:09.540
I mean, it turns
out it's not going

01:07:09.540 --> 01:07:11.550
to matter what it is too much.

01:07:11.550 --> 01:07:14.890
And then we degrade the slope
by multiplying by alpha.

01:07:14.890 --> 01:07:16.830
So here, alpha is maybe a third.

01:07:16.830 --> 01:07:17.790
I just made it up.

01:07:17.790 --> 01:07:20.530
And that would be this line.

01:07:20.530 --> 01:07:22.890
So if you take a really
small step length,

01:07:22.890 --> 01:07:24.390
you're definitely
below this line.

01:07:24.390 --> 01:07:26.557
Because if you take a really
small step length here,

01:07:26.557 --> 01:07:28.680
this is actually a
really good approximation

01:07:28.680 --> 01:07:31.770
of how much the function
is going to go down, right?

01:07:31.770 --> 01:07:38.040
And then the rule is you have
to be below this dashed line,

01:07:38.040 --> 01:07:40.270
this degraded predicted line.

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:41.970
And so the way that
works is you might

01:07:41.970 --> 01:07:45.570
start here and query the
function and find this value.

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:46.562
And you're above this.

01:07:46.562 --> 01:07:49.020
In fact, the most dramatic one
is you'd start way out here.

01:07:49.020 --> 01:07:50.730
And you're over here.

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:53.920
And actually, when you do x
plus delta x in that case,

01:07:53.920 --> 01:07:58.110
you actually have a higher value
than f of x Everybody see that?

01:07:58.110 --> 01:08:01.560
So that would be a particularly
poor choice of step length,

01:08:01.560 --> 01:08:02.070
right?

01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:07.110
So what you do, then,
is you multiply this

01:08:07.110 --> 01:08:08.710
by some factor beta.

01:08:08.710 --> 01:08:11.040
Beta is typically 1/2.

01:08:11.040 --> 01:08:12.150
People use that.

01:08:12.150 --> 01:08:13.470
They use 0.1.

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:15.780
So you simply say,
OK, that was too much.

01:08:15.780 --> 01:08:18.300
So you go back by 1/2,
and you land here.

01:08:18.300 --> 01:08:19.140
You try this one.

01:08:19.140 --> 01:08:20.200
You get this value.

01:08:20.200 --> 01:08:22.548
It's less than this
thing, and you accept it.

01:08:22.548 --> 01:08:24.090
So you take the
first one you accept.

01:08:24.090 --> 01:08:25.240
That's backtracking.

01:08:27.939 --> 01:08:29.470
Now, you know that
your intuition--

01:08:29.470 --> 01:08:33.490
actually, the story of a lot of
this unconstrained minimization

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:35.890
is, I guess, there'll
be several times

01:08:35.890 --> 01:08:39.279
when something you
would think is right

01:08:39.279 --> 01:08:40.819
is just wrong, completely wrong.

01:08:40.819 --> 01:08:43.037
So now I'll tell
you the first one.

01:08:43.037 --> 01:08:44.620
You would look at
this, and you'd say,

01:08:44.620 --> 01:08:47.080
it's, obviously,
clearly, it's better

01:08:47.080 --> 01:08:49.060
to do an exact line search.

01:08:49.060 --> 01:08:50.920
That's totally obvious, right?

01:08:50.920 --> 01:08:52.390
Because you're
actually minimizing

01:08:52.390 --> 01:08:53.660
the function along this line.

01:08:53.660 --> 01:08:54.160
Right?

01:08:54.160 --> 01:08:55.000
This makes sense.

01:08:55.000 --> 01:08:57.580
And this crude thing
that stops when

01:08:57.580 --> 01:09:00.069
you've gotten some
acceptable-- by the way, people

01:09:00.069 --> 01:09:03.430
use numbers like
alpha equals 0.01,

01:09:03.430 --> 01:09:06.220
meaning if you get even
just the slightest decrease,

01:09:06.220 --> 01:09:07.390
like, good, fine, thanks.

01:09:07.390 --> 01:09:08.080
We'll take it.

01:09:08.080 --> 01:09:10.160
So everybody see
what I'm saying?

01:09:10.160 --> 01:09:14.560
So it looks very
unsophisticated, right?

01:09:14.560 --> 01:09:17.350
Anyway, all right, now I'll
tell you the punch line.

01:09:17.350 --> 01:09:20.229
The punch line is, it
doesn't make any difference,

01:09:20.229 --> 01:09:22.990
that in fact, exact
line search is not only

01:09:22.990 --> 01:09:24.490
not better in practice.

01:09:24.490 --> 01:09:26.729
It's often worse.

01:09:26.729 --> 01:09:28.890
And the simple
dumb thing actually

01:09:28.890 --> 01:09:30.870
works just fine, thank you.

01:09:30.870 --> 01:09:32.490
This is not obvious, right?

01:09:32.490 --> 01:09:39.899
So OK, now we get to a
gradient descent method.

01:09:39.899 --> 01:09:41.330
This is the simplest one.

01:09:41.330 --> 01:09:44.430
Remember, we had to
choose a direction.

01:09:44.430 --> 01:09:47.210
I mean, I would hope
it would be the first--

01:09:47.210 --> 01:09:50.750
it should be the first
thing you would think of.

01:09:50.750 --> 01:09:52.640
Someone says, make me
an iterative method.

01:09:52.640 --> 01:09:53.569
I meant f.

01:09:53.569 --> 01:09:54.470
I meant x.

01:09:54.470 --> 01:09:56.300
And I evaluate f at that point.

01:09:56.300 --> 01:09:57.680
And I evaluate a gradient.

01:09:57.680 --> 01:10:01.980
Now, gradient is pointing in the
direction of fastest increase

01:10:01.980 --> 01:10:02.480
of f.

01:10:02.480 --> 01:10:05.480
If I move in that direction,
f goes up as fast as possible.

01:10:05.480 --> 01:10:08.510
If I move in the negative
gradient descent direction,

01:10:08.510 --> 01:10:11.540
f goes down as
quickly as possible.

01:10:11.540 --> 01:10:15.290
And not only would
you come up with this,

01:10:15.290 --> 01:10:21.060
but you'd also ask yourself,
why would you do anything else?

01:10:21.060 --> 01:10:23.690
I mean, you realize what--
think about if you didn't

01:10:23.690 --> 01:10:27.270
do the gradient method, what
happens is you arrive here,

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:29.270
and someone says, where's
the negative gradient?

01:10:29.270 --> 01:10:30.588
You go, that direction.

01:10:30.588 --> 01:10:32.130
You'd say, well,
what does that mean?

01:10:32.130 --> 01:10:33.350
You go, if you go
in that direction,

01:10:33.350 --> 01:10:35.308
you're going to go down
as quickly as possible.

01:10:35.308 --> 01:10:37.730
And you go, cool, which
way do you recommend I go?

01:10:37.730 --> 01:10:39.920
And you go, um, that way.

01:10:39.920 --> 01:10:41.900
You're like, what?

01:10:41.900 --> 01:10:43.550
Why would you recommend that?

01:10:43.550 --> 01:10:45.930
Anyway, you will
shortly see why.

01:10:45.930 --> 01:10:48.680
But it's non-intuitive.

01:10:48.680 --> 01:10:49.520
But we'll get to it.

01:10:49.520 --> 01:10:51.728
Anyway, so gradient method
is the most intuitive one.

01:10:51.728 --> 01:10:54.680
It looks like this.

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:57.320
It doesn't matter what
line search you use.

01:10:57.320 --> 01:10:58.280
And here it is.

01:10:58.280 --> 01:11:01.100
And you can prove convergence
of this and all that stuff.

01:11:01.100 --> 01:11:02.330
That's fine.

01:11:02.330 --> 01:11:07.640
It turns out this is
often unbelievably slow.

01:11:07.640 --> 01:11:11.847
So in fact, we'll see what other
search directions can actually

01:11:11.847 --> 01:11:12.930
dramatically improve this.

01:11:12.930 --> 01:11:15.510
But this is slow, very weird.

01:11:15.510 --> 01:11:17.670
And we'll look at a
picture to understand it.

01:11:17.670 --> 01:11:19.350
So I just take a quadratic.

01:11:19.350 --> 01:11:20.940
It looks like this.

01:11:20.940 --> 01:11:24.510
And here are some level curves
of the quadratic down here.

01:11:24.510 --> 01:11:26.730
And gamma is a number--

01:11:26.730 --> 01:11:27.750
oh, yeah, let's see.

01:11:27.750 --> 01:11:33.000
So gamma allows me to change the
eccentricity of the level sets,

01:11:33.000 --> 01:11:33.540
right?

01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:38.640
So if gamma equals 1, this is
1/2 x1 squared plus x2 squared.

01:11:38.640 --> 01:11:41.070
Oh, yeah, by the way, what's
the gradient in that case?

01:11:41.070 --> 01:11:45.620
If the level sets are
just circles, you're here,

01:11:45.620 --> 01:11:49.705
what does the
gradient look like?

01:11:49.705 --> 01:11:50.580
What is the gradient?

01:11:54.160 --> 01:11:56.920
In that case, this is actually--
well, I'll draw this even.

01:11:56.920 --> 01:11:58.640
It's fine.

01:11:58.640 --> 01:12:03.260
So here, here's your function.

01:12:03.260 --> 01:12:04.490
Here are the level sets.

01:12:04.490 --> 01:12:06.160
Here's 0, right?

01:12:06.160 --> 01:12:07.150
These are level sets.

01:12:07.150 --> 01:12:08.050
You're here.

01:12:08.050 --> 01:12:10.930
The gradient points
directly out like that,

01:12:10.930 --> 01:12:12.880
and the negative
gradient points directly

01:12:12.880 --> 01:12:14.950
towards 0, which
is the minimizer.

01:12:14.950 --> 01:12:20.260
So if I asked you to minimize
x1 squared plus x2 squared,

01:12:20.260 --> 01:12:23.173
using the gradient method
with an exact line search,

01:12:23.173 --> 01:12:25.465
tell me about how well that
algorithm is going to work.

01:12:29.220 --> 01:12:30.150
[? Very well. ?]

01:12:30.150 --> 01:12:31.860
Yeah, very well,
in one step, right?

01:12:31.860 --> 01:12:34.180
It's going to say,
which way should I go?

01:12:34.180 --> 01:12:36.960
You say, steepest descent, which
just happens, in this case,

01:12:36.960 --> 01:12:39.900
to aim directly at the solution.

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:40.400
Right?

01:12:40.400 --> 01:12:42.608
Then you do an exact line
search, and it's game over.

01:12:42.608 --> 01:12:43.560
Everybody got that?

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:44.580
OK.

01:12:44.580 --> 01:12:47.080
But now, that makes sense.

01:12:47.080 --> 01:12:50.100
And in fact, someone who was
proposing a gradient method

01:12:50.100 --> 01:12:52.590
would say, yeah, look
how well that works.

01:12:52.590 --> 01:12:53.347
That's great.

01:12:53.347 --> 01:12:54.430
Why would you not do that?

01:12:54.430 --> 01:12:58.620
Why would you go in a
crazy direction like that?

01:12:58.620 --> 01:13:00.910
Right?

01:13:00.910 --> 01:13:03.600
But we can actually
control the eccentricity

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:08.400
by making gamma either
really big or really small.

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:10.260
And in this case,
you can actually

01:13:10.260 --> 01:13:13.728
work out analytically what
the iterates are, right?

01:13:13.728 --> 01:13:15.520
And you get something
that looks like this.

01:13:15.520 --> 01:13:17.130
It's actually a very good
picture to have in your mind.

01:13:17.130 --> 01:13:19.738
Of course, it's totally
silly because we don't care

01:13:19.738 --> 01:13:21.030
about two-dimensional problems.

01:13:21.030 --> 01:13:24.300
We care about problems with
a minimum dimension of 200,

01:13:24.300 --> 01:13:26.130
2,000, 20,000, 2 million.

01:13:26.130 --> 01:13:29.150
But nevertheless, this
gives you the picture.

01:13:29.150 --> 01:13:32.650
So here, this is when it's
just slightly eccentric.

01:13:32.650 --> 01:13:34.570
And here, these are
the level curves.

01:13:34.570 --> 01:13:39.010
And so the negative gradient
is just simply the gradient.

01:13:39.010 --> 01:13:41.200
These are orthogonal
to the level curves.

01:13:41.200 --> 01:13:43.500
And it says, go
in that direction.

01:13:43.500 --> 01:13:46.963
This is the minimum because
we're doing exact line search.

01:13:46.963 --> 01:13:48.630
And then it says,
which way should I go?

01:13:48.630 --> 01:13:51.370
And you go like that.

01:13:51.370 --> 01:13:55.080
So people refer to
this as zig-zagging.

01:13:55.080 --> 01:13:58.110
We can make it arbitrarily bad.

01:13:58.110 --> 01:14:01.440
We can take gamma
equals 1,000, and you'll

01:14:01.440 --> 01:14:05.340
find out that this thing is
basically, going like this.

01:14:05.340 --> 01:14:06.990
And actually, I
think there's even

01:14:06.990 --> 01:14:08.790
a name in Western optimization.

01:14:08.790 --> 01:14:11.820
It's called the Maratos Effect.

01:14:11.820 --> 01:14:15.120
But the other thing I like is
it's just called zig-zagging.

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:17.100
Everybody got this?

01:14:17.100 --> 01:14:19.300
So by the way, when
you see this picture,

01:14:19.300 --> 01:14:23.220
you can figure out
some things immediately

01:14:23.220 --> 01:14:24.490
that would be pretty good.

01:14:24.490 --> 01:14:24.990
Right?

01:14:24.990 --> 01:14:27.600
So if you're in EE
or any other field,

01:14:27.600 --> 01:14:30.720
you would say, you're kind of
going in the right direction,

01:14:30.720 --> 01:14:33.030
on average.

01:14:33.030 --> 01:14:33.630
Right?

01:14:33.630 --> 01:14:34.800
I mean, the problem
is you're just

01:14:34.800 --> 01:14:37.020
doing this weird thermal
thing up and down like this.

01:14:37.020 --> 01:14:39.310
Whereas, you want to
really be going that way.

01:14:39.310 --> 01:14:41.010
And so you'd say, you know what?

01:14:41.010 --> 01:14:42.930
You should run those
search directions

01:14:42.930 --> 01:14:44.860
through a low-pass
filter, or something.

01:14:44.860 --> 01:14:47.290
I mean, again, I'm
speaking dialect.

01:14:47.290 --> 01:14:50.860
Or if you do physics, you'd say,
yeah, add some momentum to it,

01:14:50.860 --> 01:14:52.870
or something like
that, or viscosity,

01:14:52.870 --> 01:14:57.280
add a viscosity term,
so that then you'd

01:14:57.280 --> 01:14:58.930
do something that
would go like that

01:14:58.930 --> 01:15:00.757
and head towards the solution.

01:15:00.757 --> 01:15:01.840
And those are all methods.

01:15:01.840 --> 01:15:04.630
We're not going to look at
those, but that's the idea.

01:15:04.630 --> 01:15:05.140
OK.

01:15:05.140 --> 01:15:09.430
So even this baby
example tells us that--

01:15:09.430 --> 01:15:12.130
oh, but actually, we get some
really good intuition here.

01:15:12.130 --> 01:15:16.870
The gradient method
works really well

01:15:16.870 --> 01:15:20.290
when the sublevel
sets of your function

01:15:20.290 --> 01:15:24.740
have low condition
number, or they're round.

01:15:24.740 --> 01:15:29.000
Because if they're round,
the negative gradient

01:15:29.000 --> 01:15:30.860
points directly to the solution.

01:15:30.860 --> 01:15:32.570
And you're going to
go there quickly.

01:15:32.570 --> 01:15:37.130
If it has high condition
number, or the sublevel sets

01:15:37.130 --> 01:15:40.340
are weirdly squished out,
like this, that kind of thing

01:15:40.340 --> 01:15:41.150
is going to happen.

01:15:41.150 --> 01:15:43.610
Everybody got that?

01:15:43.610 --> 01:15:46.080
So that's the picture here.

01:15:46.080 --> 01:15:48.708
I mean, that's actually a
super important observation

01:15:48.708 --> 01:15:50.750
because we're going to
use that-- that's actually

01:15:50.750 --> 01:15:55.850
the segue to, basically,
Thursday's topic.

01:15:55.850 --> 01:16:00.100
OK, all right, so here's
just some examples.

01:16:00.100 --> 01:16:05.500
This is a silly function
that's non-quadratic.

01:16:05.500 --> 01:16:09.197
And this happens if you use
a backtracking line search.

01:16:09.197 --> 01:16:10.530
And here's an exact line search.

01:16:10.530 --> 01:16:12.780
And you'd look at these two,
and you'd go, like, well,

01:16:12.780 --> 01:16:14.850
I told you exact line
search was way better.

01:16:14.850 --> 01:16:17.190
But this just
shows you the idea.

01:16:17.190 --> 01:16:20.610
But it shows you that in
real, non-real examples,

01:16:20.610 --> 01:16:22.770
you're actually getting
this Maratos or zigzagging

01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:23.820
effect, right?

01:16:23.820 --> 01:16:26.430
And then we can look
at that over here

01:16:26.430 --> 01:16:28.570
and see what that looks like.

01:16:28.570 --> 01:16:33.840
So this looks like this, right?

01:16:33.840 --> 01:16:35.250
And let me explain what this is.

01:16:35.250 --> 01:16:37.980
These are the iterations.

01:16:37.980 --> 01:16:42.300
This is a log scale telling you,
actually, your suboptimality.

01:16:42.300 --> 01:16:43.650
Right?

01:16:43.650 --> 01:16:47.820
And well, you see all sorts
of weird things here, right?

01:16:47.820 --> 01:16:51.240
The first thing is that,
actually, between 0 and 100

01:16:51.240 --> 01:16:54.990
iterations, for whatever reason,
the backtracking line search

01:16:54.990 --> 01:16:56.700
was doing better.

01:16:56.700 --> 01:16:58.060
I don't know why, but it was.

01:16:58.060 --> 01:16:59.620
And this is common.

01:16:59.620 --> 01:17:01.550
So this is what it looks like.

01:17:01.550 --> 01:17:04.750
The other thing you see
is that this gets linear.

01:17:04.750 --> 01:17:06.850
And that's referred to
as linear convergence.

01:17:06.850 --> 01:17:09.550
But that's a weird
use of linear.

01:17:09.550 --> 01:17:16.450
Because it's linear on a log
or a semi-log plot, right?

01:17:16.450 --> 01:17:18.490
Because this is a log thing.

01:17:18.490 --> 01:17:22.810
What linear convergence here
means is that each step you do,

01:17:22.810 --> 01:17:26.170
each iteration reduces
the error by some factor.

01:17:26.170 --> 01:17:29.890
It's pretty small, frankly,
but we could work out

01:17:29.890 --> 01:17:31.000
what it is, right?

01:17:31.000 --> 01:17:34.810
If you divided it by--

01:17:34.810 --> 01:17:37.720
well, let's just
say that hit here.

01:17:37.720 --> 01:17:40.390
To go from 1 to
10 to the minus 4,

01:17:40.390 --> 01:17:43.090
that would take you
170 iterations, right?

01:17:43.090 --> 01:17:47.140
And that tells you
take 10 to the 4,

01:17:47.140 --> 01:17:50.050
and you take that
to the 1/175 power.

01:17:50.050 --> 01:17:53.980
And that tells you how much your
error gets smaller each step,

01:17:53.980 --> 01:17:54.550
right?

01:17:54.550 --> 01:17:57.880
So that tells you
roughly what it is.

01:17:57.880 --> 01:18:02.410
OK, so that's called
linear convergence.

01:18:02.410 --> 01:18:05.590
OK, I think maybe what
we'll do is I won't even

01:18:05.590 --> 01:18:08.360
start the next section.

01:18:08.360 --> 01:18:12.110
But this is a good
place to stop.

01:18:12.110 --> 01:18:15.460
And we'll continue next time.

